Comrades, 

I recently lent my TK50 tape drive to a colleague, so that she could send
some files describing a computational problem she was having to the
producers of her code.  Since I was in a hurry to go elsewhere, I connected
it to her workstation and left her to it, with a plea not to force the door
open because it was such a delicate beast.  True to the Bluebeard legend,
she appeared an hour later saying she had removed the tape before it was
unspooled, pushed it back in again but it didn't work and could I come and
untangle the resulting mess? 

I dismantled the drive and salvaged the tape.  When I later reconnected the
mechanism and reassembled it the Law of Amateur Repairs was invoked and I
have a spring left over!  Note that the drive seems to work perfectly well
without it, but I'd rather find out where it should go.  Apologies for the
following rather cryptic description; those who have never seen the inside
of a TK50 drive should probably stop here. 

There seem to be five main springs in the drive bay area.  Two of them
apply force to the mechanism that presents the hook of the drive's length
of tape to the catch of the TK50 tape-end;  one provides a torque, the
other is stretched between two anchoring points on the mechanism. Two other
springs, identical to one another, are compressed under the drive bay. They
push the clutch-plate up into position when the drive bay door is closed. 

My mystery spring is 24mm long, 7mm (external) diameter and consists of
exactly eight turns of wire. It is slightly stiffer than the two for the
clutch plate. The two ends are ground flat so that it stands normal to
whatever surface it is placed on.  The drive type is TK50Z-G3, built in May
1990; the label on the tape drive module inside is SP026P2054.  Whether
this is a serial number or a version number I have no idea, so I'm giving
it anyway. 

So, does anyone out there know where it should go?  Thanks in advance if
you got this far down! 

