DIGITAL ALPHA AXP SYSTEM SETS NEW TPC-A PRICE/PERFORMANCE RECORD

                    DEC 4000 Model 720 AXP System
             Provides $4,861-Per-Transaction Performance


Digital Equipment Corporation reported new results of the Transaction Processing
Performance Council Benchmark A (TPC-A) that set a new industry
price/performance record. A two-processor DEC 4000 Model 720 AXP system running
the OpenVMS operating system and the DEC Rdb database delivered 402.76 tpsA
(transactions per second) at a cost of $4,861 per tpsA. 

These price/performance results set a new record that beats all other
database servers, including PC-class systems.  In addition to the DEC
4000 Model 720 AXP server, the benchmark configuration included six
MicroVAX 3100 Model 90 client systems running the ACMS transaction
processing monitor to handle 4,030 terminals.  TPC calls this a
client/server configuration.

Digital previously announced the highest performance achieved using a
relational database: 1,079.07 tpsA at $5,850/tpsA on the DEC 7000
Model 660 AXP system running the ORACLE7 database and the ACMS TP
monitor on the OpenVMS operating system.

The TPC-A benchmark is designed to measure complete system
performance by simulating an on-line transaction processing (OLTP)
environment in which multiple users of terminals, workstations, or PCs
access and update a common database over local area networks.

