	   I'm talking EXACTLY that.  You have a server.  It runs DOS.  You
   run Novell on DOS.  You tell Novel which DOS disks to share to the network.
   Okay, maybe OS/2 instead of DOS.  Yes, it's called what you said, but it
   is NOT the base operating system.  It is a network layer residing on DOS,
   much like TCP/IP is a network layer running on Unix/VMS/...

If you run NetWare, you quickly realize that it uses DOS for a boot
loader.  After that, you can remove it from memory if you like.
Hardly DOS in any sense of the word.  In addition, NetWare disks are
not DOS disks, you can't read them or write them from DOS.

(As a historical note, Netware v2.15 didn't use DOS at all: everything
from bootblocks to OS was Novell's)

