It has been already said in this thread that VMS is actually Unix,
given its standards compliance. I forget the branding, but it is 
certainly easy enough to find -- it was about 50 or 75 messages ago.
According to that branding, VMS is currently the only real Unix 
operating system, being that VMS is the only operating system which
has been branded as such.

>  I can install Unix
>on a Vax and make the VAX disk available to DOS PCs via NFS.  So does
>that mean my Vax running Unix is running Unix on top of DOS. 

You cannot run DOS on top of a VAX. It is physically impossible,
so this is irrelevant. SoftPC does not count because it is not on 
top of the VAX; it is on top of VMS which is on top of the VAX.

> Or
>better yet, does that mean my Vax running Unix is really running Unix
>on top of VMS?

No, because VMS doesn't even enter into this picture. If you have
Ultrix installed on a VAX, then there is no room for VMS to enter into
the picture, unless it is installed on a separate disk. If that's the 
case then it is still irrelevant because you can't have VMS emulated
under Ultrix.


