In article <2m8tlc$272@augean.eleceng.adelaide.edu.au>
idall@augean.eleceng.adelaide.edu.au (Ian Dall) writes:
>In the early eighties, most educational institutions *had* VMS for
>their vaxes. But they installed unix. The unix may have been free,
>but so, in effect, was VMS (because they already had it).
>
>Also in this period VMS predominated over Unix. Then the boot was
>on the other foot. People tended to choose DEC and VMS because it
>was what they knew. Inspite of that handicap, unix overtook VMS
>in popularity.

You both point this out.  This is where numbers alone do not tell the story
and where you miss the analysis made by some of us.  It wasn't the numbers
of people who were familiar, I came from an institution running RSTS, it
was the computer science majors graduating from major universities:
the Berkeley's, then the CMUs and MITs to a lesser extent.  They needed
to study OS design, and VMS source was not cheap or openly available,
it was complex.  These students graduated and went to work for computer
start ups who founded the first workstation companies (based on the
Alto: SUN, SGI, NeXT, Apollo, and the minisupercomputer companies:
Elxsi, Convex, Alliant, Crayettes, etc.).  Nolan Bushnell of Atari
himself pointed out the shortsightedness of the analysis, he said:
	Engineers run the boat.
Additionally, with the rise of Ethernet and TCP/IP, and the DARPA contact
for 4.2 BSD by Fabry and Joy, and the decision to kill DEC-20 follow-ons
actually helped (a DEC-20 follow-on might have delayed or helped, it
could be argued both ways).  User Departments might buy computers, but
system developers hae even more sway.  Customers are not always "right"
in this business as you can ask Bill Gates.  You could watch the CS Depts
fall in line once DARPA got behind BSD.  Caltech was an amusing exception
to watch.

>This is no doubt part of the snow ball effect. However, it doesn't make
>sense to attribute all unix success to this effect.

Right: workstations, networks, minisupercomputers, and WYSIWYG GUIs
all had their influences.

My analogy of the situation is like the second Star Wars (tm) movie.
The Empire blows up the rebel power plant (Universities churning out
CS degrees) and all the little soldiers in the snow have no power
(aren't hiring students who understand the internals of their systems
like EXEC*8, MCP, OS/32, PRIMEOS), those companies are now dying (freezing)
or switching.

"Lord Vader, you can come down to the surface now."

Another analogy was a recent war in the Republic of Chad between tanks
and Toyotas with anti-tank rockets.

>The interminable debate about features that this discussion has
>degenerated into is largely beside the point. If VMS is so
>great, why did it loose market share? What could possibly
>reverse the trend?

1) It's internal structure was too complex to easily port.
Our users wanted a Cray-2 running VMS.  We had to explain to them
a little about computer OSes and architecture.
2) It was tied too closely to a 32-bit architecture of a very proprietary
nature.  They had enough problems trying to bring people out of a 16-bit
market place (and DECnet) to be concern with their future.
3) DEC did not capture enough (soon enough) of a market away from IBMs
(360/370 line) to make a sufficient impact to sustain its technical
momentum.

>Certainly the slowness of the machines which supported VMS is part of
>the story, but it doesn't explain all the sites which threw away VMS
>to run unix on the *same* machines.

RISC based workstations and what Gordon Bell called Multis started
challenging DEC pre-eminence to performance.  He wrote a paper in Science
and at the ACM Conference on the History of Personal Workstations.
His trail of companies having left DEC will tell you some of his ideas.

The next ten years isn't so clear.  DOS and MacOS going to continue to
take on Unix features.  They must to get the networking and slightly more
robust file system support (I am trying to recover data from a major data
loss).  OS/2, NT, and Pink all make great claims.  U*x will also continue
to change, the problem is that networks are too new to make guesses.
CS Depts. are under budget crunches and the economy isn't nearly as
stable.  Good luck guessing.

Please by all means keep VMS proprietary, complex, and on multi-user
superminicomputers like VAX-11/780s. 8^)  And keep certain classes
of users like Carl on it. 8^)  Nothing personal Carl, I'm certain you
would love to run VMS forever.

