Thom Ness <tness@sedist.cray.com> wrote:
>In article <TARJEIJ.94Mar21155647@ulrik.uio.no> tarjeij@ulrik.uio.no (Tarjei Jensen) writes:
>Are they really going to be sucessful supporting three operating systems?
>If they can can not, then DOES VMS HAVE A FUTURE?
>>


The amount of focus that gets put on this relative to Digital never ceases to
amaze me.  HP is supporting MPE, HP/UX, NextStep, DOS/Windows, and will
very likely do NT.  IBM has more OS's than I have fingers and toes.  Now, why
is it that only Digital will go broke with a multi-OS strategy?  And why is
it that only Digital gets slammed for this in the press?

I'm not quite sure when offering customers freedom of choice in their
purchasing decisions became a negative thing.  If VMS dies, it will be because
customers decide not to buy it.  The same is true of NT and UNIX.  Digital
has (wisely, in my opinion) decided to hedge the bets by offering customers
all the popular choices, and letting the market decide.

By the way, Digital doesn't support NT in the same sense as VMS and UNIX -
NT is a Microsoft product, and Microsoft is the primary development arm
for it.  It's not like we're footing the bill for the development of NT.

