Yup, you read it right. The PDP-11 is *not* dead.
Well, it's kinda dead in the sense that the books
I could find on it in the CPSC library here are
pretty dreadful intro-to-assembly-programming-on-
the-PDP-11-and-IBM-360 type things that don't tell
you what you want to know. To whit:

^ What exactly is the complete instruction set for
  the PDP-11/10. What extensions were added for the
  PDP-11/45.

^ A precise description of the addressing modes.
  The books I've got are plenty vague on just what
  is happening. Hell, I *know* just what MOV @(R1)+,6
  does, (including the encoding of the extra word and
  the use of the PC to grab the 6), I just want to hear
  it from soneone else.

I checked the FTP site (gatekeeper) but lo-and-behold,
the PDP-11 is no longer worthy of an entry in the
performance directory, or anywhere else I could find.
(There's some SPD's in the full index, but that's it.)

A paraphrase: is there a copy of, say, the original
DEC specifications for the PDP-11 on the net? Or
an assembly-programmer's guide? Anything? Heck,
if you have a PDP-11 simulator lying about, send
me the source code, by all means. 'cause that's
part of what I need.

------------------------------------------------------

On another note, is there similar type stuff on
the net for the Alpha? Something along the lines
of the SPARC (boo! hiss!) manual --- something
about the assembly language and the design decisions
that went into the chip. Probably this is more
sensitive stuff than the PDP-11 (ie. people can
still make money by writing books about the Alphao)
but it's worth a try.


