Ah.  In that case, he might want to look for a package called LIBSEARCH.  If I
recall correctly, it tells you what library entries contain specified keywords.
Combining that with a trivial command procedure should give the equivalent of
man -k:  Use LIBSEARCH to get the topic names, outputting them to a file.  Open
the file, read the results from LIBSEARCH, and use HELP/NOPROMPT for each of
the topics.  I've never used the package myself, so I could be mistaken as to
either its function, whether its output is convenient for what I describe, or
both.

=For me it wasn't
=at all obvious that I should look for string manipulation in the lexicals,
=till somebody showed me.

$ HELP HINTS Command_procedures

HINTS

  Command_procedures

       Performing operations specific to command procedures.

     @                 Describes how to invoke command procedures.
     Lexical           Describes functions that can be used in expressions.
				....

=  As long as you are a begginer, VMS help is terrible. If you are an interm+
=VMS user, HELP is pretty good. If you are advanced+, help is not good again.

HELP is adequate for my needs about 99% of the time.  Of course, I'm familiar
enough with VMS that I don't have to have the ITEM_LIST_3 structure described
to me every time I encounter a mention of it in HELP.

=A manager should open the manuals (which are excellent) many times till (s)he
=learn a significant part of it.

Agreed.

=  The pal from Princeton was looking for something that helps him to get
=to the int level. 

HELP does that.  It's the intermediate level where it's not adequate:  Where
you know enough to realize that you probably ought to use a system service for
something, and HELP will tell you which system service you want, but doesn't
give you detailed description of the item codes, iosbs, and so forth, and you
still haven't twigged to the fact that you can get a list of item codes by
looking at the appropriate modules in STARLET.MLB or LIB.MLB, and can
frequently get descriptions of what the item codes do by looking at the on-line
help for the corresponding lexical.
