In article <martineau-240394072043@macmartineau.ccr.hydro.qc.ca> martineau@MacMartineau.ccr.hydro.qc.ca (Alain Martineau) writes:
>I know VMS more than Unix, but am involved in purchasing an OSF/1
>based system. The integrator says that you don't need a defragmentation
>program under OSF/1 as you do with VMS. The system
>would use ADVFS.
>
>Anyone can comment that ?

The default file system that ships with OSF/1 (ufs, based on the Berkley
FFS) has an file/block allocation policy that tends to avoid fragmentation. 
Nothing particularily special about OSF/1 in this regard, many UNIXen at least 
offer a Berkley FFS as an option. No defragmenter required, none available.

AdvFS on the other hand does allow fragmentation to occur, the AdvFS Utilities
(the extra cost bits of AdvFS) include an online defragmenter in V2.0.

