I don't object to marketing, with an appropriate warning, I guess. But when
that marketing includes what I consider to be pretty much an outright lie, I
*do* object, and quite a bit, at that.  See below.

>[...]
>Advantages over host software based solutions:
>[...]
>
>* less overhead on host.  This approach requires no additional host overhead.
>The other method creates more overhead, especially as a larger
>number of users log on.

This is extraordinarily misleading and, I think, if not false advertising then
damned close to it.

You claim that an additional telnetd/rlogind per user consititutes less
overhead than the use of a login session protocol which multiplexes
connections?  

Yeah, sure.  I guess that's why so much work is being done on terminal-session
multiplexers over TCP, like Livingston's and that newfangled standard one that
so bothersomely showed up to make your suggestion even more dubious, huh?

Because they create more overhead?  Why, sure, Mr. Salesman, you said it, so
it must be true, huh?

There are a lot of good reasons not to use LAT.  Overhead is most decidedly
*not* one of them.  Using LAT instead of telnet or rlogin can dramatically
increase the performance of many large multiuser systems, just like using
a TCP-based terminal session multiplexer, in-kernel or out-of-kernel can.

Note that I don't use LAT on any machine I run at the moment, nor do I use a
TCP-based session multiplexer.

But it's not for overhead reasons...
