In article <2mvbkn$9q7@news.hk.net>,
Ken Adler (Penril Datability Networks (PDN)) <ken@hk.net> wrote:
>
>In-reply-to: tls@panix.com's message of 25 Mar 1994 03:21:49 -0500    
>
>Thor:
>
>Sorry to have miffed you.  Perhaps I did not go into the tech details of
>this set-up enough for your liking.  Also, perhaps you misunderstand
>the mechanics of how the lat/telnet translation works
>on the VCP and other like products such as the cisco offerings:
>
>>You claim that an additional telnetd/rlogind per user consititutes less
>>overhead than the use of a login session protocol which multiplexes
>>connections? 
>
>Never made that claim.  With the use of these products there is only
>a single login on the host per user be it telnet or lat.  Once again
>my point was focused on host overhead.  And no, this is not a
>"LAT/telnet is better discussion".

Yes, and you manage to rather neatly miss the point of why something like LAT
can be such a big win in terms of overhead on the host:  Because with a telnet
or rlogin connection, you've got sitting there in memory (and passing bytes
around, back and forth across the user/kernel space boundary multiple times,
etc.) one telnetd or rlogind per user.

With LAT, you don't.  Either the kernel handles everything, or one server
process (if it's implemented in user space) handles everything.  You don't
have to deal with this extra process kicking around for every user, doing a
lot of I/O and causing a lot of expensive context switches and taking up a
process slot and space in memory.

This is one of the reasons that TMux interests so many people.  Various
implementation details of LAT are really, really stupid, but the basic idea is
much better than that of telnetd/rlogind, _because_ there's so much less
overhead _for the host that's the endpoint of all of those connections_!

>In any event, based on my expereince with real live users(tm), they say
>that on healthy networks, whatever additional traffic is created by
>breaking the session into two parts  (i.e. decserver to NPT/NPT to
>IP host) does not noticeably impact the network.  Basically,
>on a performance level, they can't see any difference (i.e. negative impact)

It is _absolutely_ _not_ a question of _network_ performance.  It's a question
of loading down the target machine of all those LAT sessions now become telnet
or rlogin sessions, which now drag performance down as a telnetd or rlogind
is required for every single user.

This is a kind of Barthesean "what goes without saying" about system
performance which is one of the reasons why taking serial ports off a host and
sticking them far away across an Ethernet is _not_ as much of a performance
win as most people would expect it to be.  With a protocol like LAT, this is
much less of an issue.


>Also, typically the ease of installation advantage as well as the cost 
>advantage are usually significant motivating factors for people who
>have the duty to build practical networks while being faced with time,
>resource and $ constraints.

Well, yes, a lot of the time people do throw their hands up in the air and do
things the wrong way because it's easier.  I do it myself on a fairly regular
basis.

That does _not_ make your unqualified statement that protocol translation
"lowers overhead" true instead of false.  It's false -- see above.

