How to improve the I/O performance of DEC 3000/600 workstations under VMS?

Here is our application: We attempt to downsize some of our
finite element analyses from THD's FUJITSU/SIEMENS S400/40
vector super computer to some DEC 3000/600 workstations  because
the particular (higly nonlinear) models do not vectorize well.

We are using the finite element code ABAQUS. The models in question
need about 400 ... 600MB `memory' and about one day of CPU time on
the FUJITSU. From experiences with ABAQUS on different computer systems
(MVS/370, VSPS/S440, VMS/VAX, UX/HP9000, RS6000/AIX) we know that
- ABAQUS' out-of-core solver is more efficient than the OS' paging
  (i.e. we better request only virtual storage < physical storage
  and let ABAQUS solve the equations using temporary disk storage);
- models about 1.001 ... 8 times larger than the available memory
  all take about the same performance hit when compared to an
  in-core solution -- which can be as low as a factor of 2 slower
  when using the solid state disk of the FUJITSU and up to a factor
  of 20 slower (typically we get a factor of 8 on VAX/VMS and HP9000/UX);
- ABAQUS uses just one `hot file', which is slightly larger than
  the model size (i.e. 430 ... 650MB);
- this file is read and written with FORTRAN DIRECT ACCESS whereby
  large chunks (> 10MB) of sequentially ordered data are accessed in random;
- we cannot change the code (binary license only).

At the current memory prices (even for non-DEC sources) we cannot
afford to stuff 512MB or even 1GB memory into the workstations --
128MB seems to be the limit.

Considering that the I/O performance will determine which fraction 
of the ALPHA CPU power we can realize   what can we do to improve it 
(under VMS) without spending too much on additional hardware?
I'm thinking about
  - host based striping
        (Is it available for VMS/AXP? Under which name? In the CSLG?
         Will it help if the striped disks are on the same SCSI adapter?)
  - the new fast-and-wide SCSI turbochannel adapter KZTSA or
    the similar CMD CTC-3000
        (Will this adapter be standard in future DEC workstations or
         will it remain an exotic item due to the PCI-bus systems on
         the horizon?
         Can state-of-art fast-and-wide disks really transmit at
         rates of 20MB/sec?
         Would a large transmit rate help in our application?
         Any more precise info (date) on VMS support other than
         ``The KZTSA will also be supported with a future release
         of the OpenVMS AXP operating system'' in biz.dec.decnews?)

Experiences and/or opinions anyone?
What else should I consider?


Thanks in advance,
Volker Koettgen                   THD, Werkstoffmechanik, Darmstadt, Germany
                                  koettgen@wmec01.wm.bauing.th-darmstadt.de


