In article <2mhrtt$po0@rs18.hrz.th-darmstadt.de>, D62J@wmec01.wm.bauing.th-darmstadt.de (Volker B. Koettgen) writes:
>          Can state-of-art fast-and-wide disks really transmit at
>          rates of 20MB/sec?

I've been playing with some drives (a couple of Seagates and a Conner)
recently; 8-bit SCSI. The Conner does, indeed, transfer data over the
SCSI bus at 10MB/s. However, drive throughput is another matter; although
the drive can burst that fast, it cannot sustain that rate. I have,
however, completely filled a Seagate Barracuda and the Conner at a
sustained rate of 2.6MB/s. The Seagate Hawk loses it on the inner tracks
at that rate. Read performance for all of the drives is better than
write performance, because I was doing sequential reads and the drives
have large caches.

> What else should I consider?

You might want to take a look at GENROCO's TURBOstor IPI controllers.
They are supposed to perform very well.
