In article <ROCKWELL.94Mar21071120@nova.umd.edu>, rockwell@nova.umd.edu (Raul Deluth Miller) writes:
> David L. Cathey:
> .  Novell is not an OS, but sometimes called a "NOS" (Network
> .  Operating System) which rides the base OS (again, typically DOS or
> .  OS/2) for the purpose of interfacing to a network.  Without the
> .  base OS, it's kindof useless.  It creates a "shell" to intercept
> .  DOS calls, as opposed to LanMan etc that uses a redirector.
> 
> Huh?
> 
> I'm talking about the thing that runs on the server, not the thing
> that runs on the clients.  NetWare 3.1 or NetWare 4, are the official
> names, I believe.

	I'm talking EXACTLY that.  You have a server.  It runs DOS.  You
run Novell on DOS.  You tell Novel which DOS disks to share to the network.
Okay, maybe OS/2 instead of DOS.  Yes, it's called what you said, but it
is NOT the base operating system.  It is a network layer residing on DOS,
much like TCP/IP is a network layer running on Unix/VMS/...

> As far as I can tell, there's absolutely no reason to believe that PC
> MS-DOS networks are typically implemented with MS-DOS on the server.
	
	If you are running a client-server application, your server _must_
be running the hardware/OS that the server software is built for.  Often,
this is DOS or OS/2.  While several OS's can support rudimentary file and
print services, there is also mail (i.e. cc:Mail), 3270 (Open Connect), and
others that REQUIRE that the server be a DOS platform - or you put in yet 
another gateway.

