NNTP-Posting-Host: post.its.mcw.edu
X-Newsreader: TIN [version 1.2 PL1]

I have been following this thread, and have only one point to say....
VMS (oops.  OPENvms) is not "dead".  It's still gonna be around
for a very very long time.  And, so are the various flavors of UNIX(tm)
that we all know and love.

EACH of these two operating systems has its pros and cons, and each
definately has its place in computing as we know it today.

I have VMS here on our campus, and I like it a lot.  It suits us quite
well for our major-class production systems.

Likewise, we ALSO have varying flavors of UNIX around here too.   A few
years ago, we didn't have ANY unix around here-- but came to realize
that certain things simply functioned better in a unix environment.

We have BOTH, and for many good reasons.  There is not, nor do I ever
see (for quite a while, anyway), a "single" operating system that
does it all.

For example, in the network world, there is NO one "super tool" for
network management.   You need various tools, from various vendors,
to do the job right.   For example, a tool like Sun NetManager is great
for detecting certain problems, like a router going out to lunch, but
is useless for detecting a cable short.  For THAT, you need a portable
cable analyzer.    The same goes for operating environments--
each institution has DIFFERENT requirements, and thus, different
solutions to best meet those needs.

As far as both unix and VMS, I hope to see them BOTH around for
a long time.   In the LONG scope of things, I suspect we'll see
the functions of both "merging" somewhat, but that's a long way off.

One is not "better" than the other, because you have to set up the
scope of requirements first.  

In our case:      Very large-sized multi-user system for HealthCare
                  billing applications.    Happens to be a vax 6000-class
                  machine running VMS.   Someday, we'll go AXP.

                  Medium-sized multi-user campuswide information server
                  (email, news, gopher, etc etc).   Happens to be
                  a unix system.   Works great.

I wouldn't force (a) to be in (b)'s platform, or vice-versa.  It just
wouldn't work out for us.

Long live (fill in your favorite OS here) !


