In article <TARJEIJ.94Mar21155647@ulrik.uio.no> tarjeij@ulrik.uio.no (Tarjei Jensen) writes:
>The ultimate nail in the VMS coffin was probably the licensing system. The way
>DEC handled it lead a lot of people to believe that they were being set up for
>milking by DEC. If they had been more careful in deciding what the system
>manager should control and what the licensor should control I think VMS would
>still be considered a vital operating system for most organisations. The point
>system killed  VMS.
>
This may be one of the factors. A very important factor, in my opinion, was
that DEC was very slow to react to the competition from the UNIX computer
vendors. When I first started working as an S.E. in computer sales, there
were many potential customers that would not even talk to us. They were
"DEC shops" and bought all of their equipment and services from DEC. 
Since DEC was very slow to respond, their price/performance became
so poor that the potential customers could no longer ignore the cost 
savings from the UNIX vendors. We used to joke that if we could offer
twice the performance for half the cost we could get a DEC shop to listen
to us. With DEC's complancency, we were able to do this.

If you believe the VMS supporters side of this thread, DEC has now moved to
a point where they are offering competitive price/performance. I have
two problems with this. The first problem is that DEC's products are 
only competitive in limited segments of the market. Primarily the low end,
where there is strong competition and margins are low. The other problem
is related. It is that DEC has still not reengineered their company to
be able to be profitable on the low margins that exist in all areas of
the computer market. I say still, because they have laid off 10's of thousands
of employees, but have not IMHO cured the problem. This is a point that I
have questioned before in this thread. It is a question about DEC's focus.
Are they really going to be sucessful supporting three operating systems?
If they can can not, then DOES VMS HAVE A FUTURE?
