Thanks, one and all, for the information on my problem.  The original problem
statement was connecting/converting a Kennedy 9400 tape drive (Pertec) to a
SCSI bus on a DEC Alpha workstation.  Here is a summary report I am turning in,
and a list of the replies I received.  In the summary report, UMDSPA is a
MicroVAX II to which the three Kennedy drives are connected, and UMDSP is a
VAXstation 4000-90 used for other projects that has a SCSI HP 9-track drive.

Larry Bleau
University of Maryland
bleau@umdsp.umd.edu
301-405-6223

==============================================================================
                Tape Drive Options For New AXP (Alpha) System

The task is to provide a means by which 9-track tapes can be read on a new DEC
AXP (Alpha) workstation.  The basic options available are:

   Option 1:  keep the MicroVAX II with its tape drives but configure it in
              some fashion to make the drives accessible
   Option 2:  connect UMDSPA's tape drives to the new AXP system
   Option 3:  make use of UMDSP's HP tape drive
   Option 4:  purchase a new tape drive.


                           DETAILED LIST OF OPTIONS

These options are expressed below in greater detail in a fairly non-technical
manner.  They can be achieved with various hardware or software solutions.
Greater detail is provided in the technical section.  

1.  Keep UMDSPA; either leave it standalone (a-c) or cluster with the AXP system (d-e)
 a. use it as tape server and compute engine
 b. use it as tape server and terminal server (no disk or compute)
 c. use it as tape server only (no interactive logins or batch jobs, no local
    disk storage) 
 d. use disk serving: UMDSPA programs can read data from and place data on the
    AXP's disk
 e. use tape serving: AXP programs can read/write to tape drives

2.  Move tape drives to AXP system
 a. convert drives to native SCSI devices
 b. purchase an interface converter to connect drives to SCSI bus

3.  Use UMDSP's tape drive, making UMDSP a tape server for the AXP system
 a. have systems separate
 b. cluster UMDSP with AXP system

4.  purchase new SCSI 9-track drive for AXP system


                                 ASSUMPTIONS

An AXP system will be purchased.  It will have sufficient disk storage to
handle the operating system, layered products, data and programs for the WIND
project, plus programs (and perhaps data) for other projects.  It will have a
cdrom device for initial system installation and an 8mm drive for backups.

A terminal server will be purchased along with the new AXP system to handle
terminal lines.  It will be needed unless we decide to go with options 1a, 1b,
1d, or 1e.  In the long run it would likely be purchased anyway, so its cost is
not included in the analysis.

Options 2-4 assume the MicroVAX II will not be available for future use.

Making two of the three tape drives available on the new AXP system will be
sufficient. 
                Tape Drive Options For New AXP (Alpha) System
                                                                                p 2
                                   ANALYSIS

