Option               Cost      Cost      Cost   tages     tages      Comments

1a tape+term+compute    0     1,950     9,970   A         a          1,10

1b tape+term srvr       0     1,950     8,420   A,C       a          1,6

1c tape server only     0     1,950     8,120   A,C       a          1,6,7

1d cluster UMDSPA+AXP   0         0     2,500   C         a,b        4

1e cluster UMDSPA+AXP 1,350       0     2,500   B,C       a,h        4,9

2a cvt drive->SCSI                0     2,500             g,i        5

2b cvt Pertec->SCSI 1,800         0     2,500             f          3

3a use UMDSP's drive    0     1,700       190   A         c,d        2,8

3b cluster UMDSP+AXP    0         0         0             c,d,e      4,8

4  buy new drive    6,500         0         0   D         c          11


NOTE:  All these options are also available for a VAX workstation.  The same
advantages, disadvantages, and comments apply.


Advantages:

A. The software solution allows full use of the served tape drive: mount
   normal, mount foreign, initialization, and backup.  I used a demonstration
   of this software some years ago and it performed as expected.  We can get a
   free 30-day evaluation of this software.  It is available on VAX and AXP. 
   It can be easily reversed/removed.
B. This would allow the tape drives to be accessed directly from the AXP.  This
   is done through a mechanism called TMSCP serving.  See note h.
C. This would save money by taking parts of the UMDSPA system off maintenance.
D. This is the cleanest solution: it avoids any uncertainty; gives you new,
   good-working equipment under warranty; and likely provides better performance.

Disadvantages:

a. Some of UMDSPA would have to remain on maintenance.
b. The tape drives would only be accessible from UMDSPA.  Users could, however,
   copy files and data to the AXP's disk.
c. Only one tape drive would be available.
d. The HP tape drive would be shared between users of UMDSP and the AXP system. 
   This could be done, but would require some scheduling.
e. As of VMS V5.5 only MU tape devices could be shared cluster-wide.  Since the
   HP drive is (a SCSI device) appears as an MK device it cannot be shared in
   the manner described.  This will be added in a future version of VMS, so this
   option might not be available for some time.
                Tape Drive Options For New AXP (Alpha) System
                                                                                p 3
                             ANALYSIS (continued)

Disadvantages (continued):

f. This would probably require some additional maintenance expertise (ie, cost),
   as it could be the source of future problems.  A very non-standard configuration.
g. This would require a length of SCSI cable to stretch from the AXP to each of
   the tape drives.  This could easily exceed SCSI cable length limits.
h. This would require the purchase of a replacement tape controller for UMDSPA,
   since none of our existing tape controllers emulate TMSCP.
i. Modifications would be drive-specific, not transferrable to non-Kennedy drives.

Comments:

1.  A server license would have to be purchased for UMDSPA, and a client license
    for the AXP.  A server license for UMDSP, to make the HP tape drive
    available to the AXP, would cost an additional $450.  Software maintenance
    fees are 15% of the purchase price ($292.50) per year, after the 90 day
    support has ended.
2.  A server license would have to be purchased for UMDSP, and a client license
    for the AXP.  Software maintenance fees are 15% of the purchase price
    ($255.00) per year, after the 90 day support has ended. 
3.  Only two drives can be connected in this manner, and they will have to be at
    the end of the SCSI bus.  These would appear as multiple SCSI devices (MKAxxx).
    This has been done before at other sites.  I can get referrals.
4.  This would form a mixed cluster, with one VAX and one AXP.  The management
    tasks for such a cluster would be greater than for a cluster with only a
    single cpu-type.  For instance, programs compiled and linked on one cluster
    member could not be run on the other cluster member
5.  The drives themselves will have to be modified.  This has been done before
    at other sites.
6.  This assumes the CDC and Century Data disks (DISKA0 and DISKA2) will be
    taken off maintenance.
7.  This assumes the three terminal controllers will be taken off maintenance
    and the terminal lines will be moved to the new terminal server.
8.  Since the HP tape drive will be on maintenance regardless its maintenance
    cost is not included here.
9.  The hardware cost can be decreased if we are willing to purchase a used tape
    controller.
10. This is UMDSPA's current configuration.
11. This is a ballpark figure only.

