From: pack@acd.ucar.edu (Daniel Packman)

9 track tape drives are rather outdated ... their storage capacity is small,
media costs are high, and drives themselves are expensive to buy and maintain.
I believe there are Q-bus to scsi boxes that could be used to transfer
your drives (3rd party), but these are expensive as well.

We had a similar problem with an old VAX 8300 with TU81 tape drive (as well
as ancient RA81 disks).  We decided to avoid 9 track drives entirely and go
with an 8mm drive.  Add up your maintenance costs and media costs per year
and see how that matches the initial outlay for a new 8mm drive and its very
small media cost.   Similarly, one could spec a 4mm DAT system.  With
built-in cd-rom, software distribution works much better now for us.  We
don't miss the 9 track drive at all.

Good luck.


