From: haynes@cats.ucsc.edu (Jim Haynes)

I looked into this a while back, as we had several Kennedy 9400 drives at the
time.  I was told that National Cash Register has a board that does a
SCSI-to-Pertec interface conversion.  I assume you have Pertec interface on
the drives, the one with two 50-conductor PC edge connectors sticking out
the back of the drive.  I reached somebody at NCR eventually, and was told
that their distributor for this stuff is Arrow Electronics.  So I called a
guy at Arrow and he said yes they had such a card.  It is not self-powered
or anything, so you'd have to cobble it up into a box with a power supply.
He was supposed to send me some literature, but never did.

Then we talked to our maintenance contractor and also to Kennedy.  Kennedy
said they didn't have anything for the 9400, but you could conver the 9610
to SCSI by adding a board, or changing a board.  So we had a 9610 and
had our maintenance contractor convert it and forgot about the 9400s.

When we bought the 9610 it was with Pertec interface.  The reason we bought it
was that at the time we wanted another 9400, and our maintenance contractor
told us they would charge considerably less per month if we got a 9610
instead of the 9400.  I've never been very happy with it - this is
independent of the SCSI issue.  The 9610 is autoload and the operators don't
like it because they can't see what it's doing and it loads so slowly
compared with the 9400s.  Then various things on it don't work quite right
and the contractor seems unable to fix them.  Like it's supposed to sense
tape density at loading, just like the 9400s do, but it quit doing that.
And it's supposed to have remote density selection just like the 9400s, but
that has quit working too.  So operators have to know what density of tape
they are trying to read and dial it in manually.

Anyway, if you persist you might run down the NCR board - I guess it's
AT&T now - and try it.  My management just wasn't very interested in
the amount of experimenting we would have to do - I mean Pertec interfaces
are all a little bit different from one another, so there was no assurance
that if we bought the board it would work.

