Bill,
 
The story is a bit more convoluted than that. Since I am not 
privy to the exact details (I heard them about third hand), I 
will not go into it in depth.
 
There have been some public discussions of porting to other 
architectures (MIPS, Intel), however, porting VMS is more than 
just a recompile for an arbitrary architecture.
 
There are implicit presumptions in VMS about the hardware 
environment. So long as these assumptions are true, a port is 
technically feasible. If they are not true, then things become 
complicated. The most obvious example is the four protection 
modes (User, Supervisor, Executive, and Kernel). You will note 
that MIPS does not have four access modes. However, ALPHA does.
 
If my memory serves me correctly, there are one or two other 
similar fundamental hardware requirements (perhaps somebody from 
OpenVMS-ALPHA Engineering will be willing to respond with the 
exact list, I believe it was discussed in a public presentation 
about two years ago).
 
Not having first person information on why Dave Cutler left 
Digital, I will not comment on it. Dave's work is often 
excellent, and many of the features in WNT are evolutions of 
facilities and concepts recognizable from other systems where he 
has been involved. However, all of us are human, and I suspect 
that as we all build a more extensive experience base with WNT, 
we will discover that WNT, like all systems has its 
shortcommings.
 
I hope that the above is helpful. If I have been unclear, please 
feel free to contact me via Email or phone.
 
- Bob
