	<SET TERM/SPEED=300>  If you have a network of PC's in a client-server
network, you will probably find that most of the servers are PC-based running
the same OS as the client PC (typically DOS/Windows or OS/2).  

> Most PC servers already run a different OS [Novell] than the PC
> clients.  Unix is looking good for third party server-add ons.  The
> Novell OS is rather touchy, difficult to upgrade.

	Novell is not an OS, but sometimes called a "NOS" (Network Operating
System) which rides the base OS (again, typically DOS or OS/2) for the purpose
of interfacing to a network.  Without the base OS, it's kindof useless.  It
creates a "shell" to intercept DOS calls, as opposed to LanMan etc that uses a
redirector.

	Not that I'm opposed to "Unix" on a PC, it's just that its got a
lot of catchup with IBM and MS to get market share, and easy availability
of shrink-wrap software.  Sure theres free stuff, etc, but Joe Public may
not have the time and patience to find it.  So what OS does he run?

	Maybe eventually, but I hope DOS dies since "DOS memory management"
is an oxymoron (or to use the technical term: Crap).

