In article <ROLFF.94Mar15120006@svea.eurokom.ie>,
Anders Rolff <rolff@svea.eurokom.ie> wrote:
>In article <1994Mar13.125410.18804@avatar.montagar.com> davidc@avatar.montagar.com (David L. Cathey) writes:
>	   Why buy a Unix system if I can run the same application on my PC for
>   half the cost?
>
>You don't. But there are quite a few applications that can't run on a DOS
>or Windows system. Systems that require multithreading, etc, require a lot
>of work on a traditional PC operating system. Hence Windows NT, which also
>gives you hardware independency.
>
>The big threat to VMS, I think, is that PCs are getting so powerful and
>robust that you can run a serious Unix system on them. And PC hardware
>is probably the most cost-effective hardware you can find.

An oversight often made when people think of PC's is that they imagine them 
only in a desktop configuration, and don't take them seriously for 
larger-scale applications.  PC's are packaged in many different 
form-factors now, and able to meet a wide variety robustness requirements.  
Since they're still logically equivalent to a desktop PC, they're able to 
run OS's and tools from the PC environment.  Even the poor I/O bandwidth 
critisms are no longer valid with PCI and VESA Localbus being common now.

As a result, desktop machines become logically equivalent for development 
purposes while the actual runtime machine is altered in whatever manner is 
needed to fit the requirements of the runtime environment.  With the 
addition of distributed and load-balancing OS's, the performance of the 
system can be scaled up and down to meet many requirements.  I've seen many 
traditional mini-computer applications (nuclear reactor monitoring, for 
example) be successfully handled by "mutant PC's" now.
