In article <1994Mar13.135241.18814@avatar.montagar.com>,
>	What functionality do I not have?  VMS has the same functionality.
>If I want to it in a pure VMS environment, the commands may be different
>but the functionality is the same.  If that doesn't make you happy, then
>you can use the POSIX environment and do it there.

  I might be wrong. I might be negligant. But once uppon a time I had to
create files, which could be send in a PSI mail over X-25, and it was also
required that a C program using open and tell, were able to know the number
of bytes that can be read from the file (in the UNIX picture it's called the
length of the file). I *think* that VMS counted the headers, bookkeeping
info, etc. and added it to the actual length, at least the number was not
correct. I could not find any file format in VMS that offered these
functionalities. What I wanted to point out: with my knowledge, with my 
practice, I wasn't able to do something that was trivial in UNIX. It
does not mean at all that UNIX is inferior. Unfortunately not everybody
knows everything about VMS. If somebody claims this, he is stupid. There
are different levels of knowledge. At a lower level the UNIX enviroment
offers more than the VMS in *some* cases. At a higher level VMS is
superior in many cases. That's why UNIX is so popular. You don't have
to be a computer expert to do things on it. This is not true for VMS.
If Joe Smith (let's say), biologist (anything but computer scientist)
wants something that offers a bit more than a PC, and he can't afford
a system manager (you can buy a decent workstation for 10-15k, you can
buy a decent VMS system admin for let's say 60-100k/year), he will obviously
go with UNIX. It will crash once a week, but that might be acceptable.
As for a central mainframe with 100 parallel users, the UNIX is just not
an option. In a such case the choice is between VMS and VM.
  Please don't compare things WITHOUT defining the enviroment. As far as
I know, I can't play Black Planet on any VMS box, but I can do it on a spectrum
(good old Sinclair). So in that enviroment, the Spectrum outperforms the
VMS. Period.


Just my 0.02,
  Gyula

