> In article <1994Mar13.125410.18804@avatar.montagar.com> davidc@avatar.montagar.com (David L. Cathey) writes:
> 	   Why buy a Unix system if I can run the same application on my PC for
>    half the cost?
> 
> You don't. But there are quite a few applications that can't run on a DOS
> or Windows system. Systems that require multithreading, etc, require a lot
> of work on a traditional PC operating system. Hence Windows NT, which also
> gives you hardware independency.

	Or they run them differently.  I've seen shops that have an application
that actually works on a small network of PC's.  True WNT is a better OS
is some respects, but it still has a slow growing market share and a still
uncertain future.

> The big threat to VMS, I think, is that PCs are getting so powerful and
> robust that you can run a serious Unix system on them. And PC hardware
> is probably the most cost-effective hardware you can find.

	The PC threat to VMS is not that they can run Unix, otherwise SCO and
Linux would have made a much bigger splash.  PC's are a threat to both VMS
and Unix since the hardware has the price/performance as even the mid-range
systems (if not individually, then networked).  The "Visual" languages,
large number of 4GL-type tools, interoperability of many diverse applications,
networking, CAD/CAM, databases, ease of finding software, availability of
literature (DOS for Dummies!), etc all make for great advantages for
DOS/Windows.  Again, it's has nothing to do with "superiority" of the OS, and
is a powerful force in the industry despite its inferiority.

	The PC Unixes are still too buggy to "bet-your-business" on, and
shrink-wrap software is limited.  DOS and friends have bugs, too, but they are
a known quantity, typically with a set of known workarounds and better
supported.

