In article <2lg3qh$419@gap.cco.caltech.edu> carl@SOL1.GPS.CALTECH.EDU writes:
>I'm not claiming that it was intended as a marketing move, merely that in
>retrospect, it worked out to be one.
>
>=	In any case, what happened during the next 14 years ? Great marketing ?
>
>No, the users who learned Unix because it was given, free, to colleges
>graduated, got jobs wherein they had the authority to choose which operating
>system to use, and chose Unix because it was the only O/S with which they were
>familiar.

This is no doubt part of the snow ball effect. However, it doesn't make
sense to attribute all unix success to this effect.

In the early eighties, most educational institutions *had* VMS for
their vaxes. But they installed unix. The unix may have been free,
but so, in effect, was VMS (because they already had it).

Also in this period VMS predominated over Unix. Then the boot was
on the other foot. People tended to choose DEC and VMS because it
was what they knew. Inspite of that handicap, unix overtook VMS
in popularity.

The interminable debate about features that this discussion has
degenerated into is largely beside the point. If VMS is so
great, why did it loose market share? What could possibly
reverse the trend?

Certainly the slowness of the machines which supported VMS is part of
the story, but it doesn't explain all the sites which threw away VMS
to run unix on the *same* machines. Now the alpha makes VMS machines
competitive on performance again but gives them no edge. OSF/1 is
available for the same machines.

Whether Record oriented file systems are a good thing or not and
whether wildcards should be expanded in the shell or not are
evidently a a matter of opinion. However, these differences
are largely the same as they always were so I still maintain
that there are no features in VMS likely to woo people back to
the fold.

And *that* was the original question. Not which was "better".
