In article <2lv1ug$gme@news.iastate.edu>, john@iastate.edu (John Hascall) writes:
>    These two are related.  The reason you can have different "mailers"
>    under Unix is because, unlike VMS, the mail system is not monolithic.
>    There is a delivery agent (e.g., "sendmail") and a user agent (e.g.,
>    "mail").  It is little surprise that looking in the user agent man page
>    for a delivery agent function is unfruitful.

	Actually, VMS is rather modular.  It's just that the delivery agent(s)
are dynamically pulled into play (via sharable images) as needed.  Also, if
you are using MX, you divide it out a little further by having a multiplexor
of delivery agents which does run externally to the MAIL command.  Also, by
using Callable MAIL, you can create your own user agents (see VMAIL) that
interface with the delivery agents as does MAIL.  PMDF also provides alternate
sets of delivery agents (FAX, SkyPage, and others) for use.

