From  article <2lugql$gg@werple.apana.org.au>, by riviera@werple.apana.org.au (Nikolas Tumbri):
> howd'y
> 
> I have just become the proud owner of an old DEC IPV11. This device is, 
> supposedly an interface between an 11/03,11/23 to the outside world ie. 
> to sensors, converts A/D, D/A, and other things.
> 
> I would like to experiment with this device, but don't know if it is 
> complete. Also the docs. supplied are VREY BASIC.
> 
> So if there is anyone (either here in australia or else wherein the world ) 
> who could assist with info, and maybe parts to get it working, I would much 
> appreciate it.
I spent years of my life trying to coax IP11 and IPV11 into working 
acceptably; even when they were, in principle, supported devices, it 
was next to impossible.  The interface cards were unreliable and the 
device drivers DEC supplied were utter crap.  IP11s had a delightful way
of doing interrupts: first the IP11 controller would interrupt the main 
CPU, then it would poll down its own bus (called the D-bus, if I recall 
correctly) to see which interface card needed servicing.  If it took too 
long to figure that out, it wouldn't respond when the device driver read 
from its CSR, causing a bus timeout in kernel mode, and thus a system 
crash.  The group in DEC that invented IP11 was dissolved back in 83 or 
so, and we could never get anyone to take responsibility for them.

If you're trying to put together a sensor-based system, I strongly 
recommend you look into third-party hardware; I had a lot of luck 
with stuff from Computer Produtcs, Inc, who are based, I think, in 
Fort Lauderdale.  They don't supply device drivers, but the register 
layouts are simple enough that it's easy to build your own, or just 
do access directly to the I/O page.  I haven't worked with PDP-11s 
for about 8 years, though, so I don't know what their current status is.

If you have to maintain an existing IP system, you have my sympathy.  

Mike


