writes...
>[...comp.org.decus trimmed...]
>Carl J Lydick <carl@SOL1.GPS.CALTECH.EDU> wrote:
>}=> VAX-11 and MicroVAX are the two previous architectures.  VAX-11 architecture
>}=> machines are no longer sold by DEC.
>}
>}Though I've always, in the past, heard them referred to as separate
>}architectures, someone from DEC has posted that they're considered to be the
>}same architecture.  As to "binary compatibility," a good part of that was
>}achieved by generating an exception on the non-VAX-11 machines when one of the
>}instructions not supported on later VAXen was seen, and calling library
>}routines to emulate the instructions. 
> 
>    The majority of VAX processors (whether tagged "MicroVAX" or not)
>    emulate some instructions (for example, the packed decimal or
>    H-float ones).

There has been in fact only one model of VAX processor which implemented in
"hardware/microcode" every instruction that was defined in the VAX architecture
during its lifetime - that was the 11/730 (and its cousin the 11/725).  All
other models omitted some set of instructions or other.  (The 11/780 and
11/750 could support the complete instruction set with an extra-cost option.)

The VAX architecture was defined with subsets right from the start.  It is
nonsense to talk of "non-VAX-11" VAX processors.  I don't doubt that some
misinformed people turned "MicroVAX instruction set" into "MicroVAX
architecture", but that doesn't make the "MicroVAX architecture" real.
There is not now and never was such a thing.  Even to talk of 
"MicroVAX instruction set" is to be sloppy, as there were three major
implementations of "MicroVAX" processors, each implementing a different
subset of instructions.

If you insist on a "MicroVAX architecture", then you have to also have
a "VAX 9000 architecture", "VAX88xx architecture", "VAX 8200 architecture",
etc.  You should also then try reading the VAX Architecture Reference
Manual to see what the real story is.

