    What big bucks?  POSIX is licensed with OpenVMS at no extra
    charge.  If you're using CDROM distribution and CDROM
    documentation you don't even need to purchase media and
    documentation.  It was the X/Open Base XPG 3 branding we received
    after bundling POSIX with VMS that caused the marketeers to
    confuse everyone by changing the name to OpenVMS.

    For Alpha AXP systems, OSF/1 and OpenVMS with POSIX have the same
    cost for hardware + O/S except, I believe, a slight premium on
    OpenVMS at the unlimited user license level.  In this day of
    Client/Server approaches I'm not seeing a whole lot of unlimited
    user license requests on either O/S.

    With OpenVMS, those users who wish to remain in the VMS world can
    do so with the DCL command interpreter, those wishing a full open
    systems approach may do so with the shell as their native
    environment, and crazies like me can switch back and forth
    dynamically within a single session.

