In article <13MAR199415421589@almach.caltech.edu>, 
shoppa@almach.caltech.edu (Timothy D. Shoppa x4256) writes...

>>There has been in fact only one model of VAX processor which implemented in
>>"hardware/microcode" every instruction that was defined in the VAX architecture
>>during its lifetime - that was the 11/730 (and its cousin the 11/725).  All
>>other models omitted some set of instructions or other.  (The 11/780 and
>>11/750 could support the complete instruction set with an extra-cost option.)

>And because of this, the 11/730 actually does REAL*8 (and COMPLEX*16)
>math faster than the 11/780 and 11/750, and many other (usually faster)
>VAXen.  (I believe the 11/730 was the fastest at REAL*8 until the introduction
>of the 88xx... but I'm sure someone will correct me.)

You meant REAL*16 (H_float).  Though the base 730 was a real dog at H_float
(as it was at most everything, though it did have its surprises), the
optional FP730 accelerated H_float so that it was the fastest until the
8600 (not 8800, that came later) was released.

The 780 (with FP780) was very good at REAL*8; more so than you'd expect
given its integer performance.  This is what made the 780 look so good
when compared to the then-current IBM 43xx models (which cost a lot
more than the $100K the 780 did.)

