In article <matuscak-100394091313@zaphod.rohrer.com>, matuscak@rohrer.com (Joe Matuscak) writes:
|>Hi. Ive just recieved a couple DECpc XL 566 systems (66mhz Pentiums). They
|>have
|>a DEC installed Diamond Viper PCI video accelerator in them. Im having a
|>problem getting these things to drive a monitor at more than 640X480. I can
|>change the setup in windows and it claims to be 1024X768, but it doesnt
|>really
|>change. Ive reinstalled the Diamond software, Ive yanked out the DE204, ive
|>tried two different monitors (a Sony 1604S and a NEC 6FGp). I also tried
|>talking to Digital :-)
|>
|>Any suggestions?
|>
|>Does anyone have one of these things working with a high resolution
|>monitor?
|>
|>Thanks.


I'm not sure about the XL series, but in the earlier DECpc LP systems, you 
have to tell the BIOS what resolution the monitor is capable of.  This is
presumably to prevent the video hardware from driving scan frequencies that
could damage a low-resolution monitor.  If you loaded a windows driver
that tried to force the video hardware to a resolution higher than what the
BIOS had been told the monitor supports, then you'd get nothing but black
on the screen.  

However, in the XL series, the VIDEO hardware is not integrated on the 
motherboard, so it's unclear whether or not this still applies.

Regards,

>>>Joe Ervin


