Matthew Liggett <mliggett@onyx.chem.indiana.edu> writes:
 
>Hi.  We're considering temporarily swapping a chunk of ~64MB (as close
>as we can get to that) from one AXP 3000 to another.  The machines are
>NOT identical.. one has a tower and the other a desktop case, and the
>2ndary model designator is different between them.  This is a
>temporary swap and I'd like to know
>(1) will I need to rebuild the kernel or something similar in order to
>have the machine recognize the new [lack of] memory?
>(2) how can i tell what memory is where inside the case?
>(3) how can i be sure i'm not going to fry something when I do all of
>this? :)
 
The DEC 3000 line has two types of memory, for the 300 series, and the
non-300 series.  As long as the desktop is not a 300, the memory
should be movable.  A more pressing concern is how dense the
packaging is of the 64 MB you are trying to move.
 
Memory mother boards hold the chips, and if the memory mother boards
are low density, you may run out of slots trying to add the 64 MB.
 
As far as frying anything, normal static electricity safeguards are
a good idea.
 
The Alpha systems handbook is a great resource for detailed physical
information on the MMB layouts.
 
To be sure you are using all the new memory added, boot /genvmunix
and run the doconfig command.  Going up in memory should not be
a problem.  I would restore the original vmunix before shutting down
to pull the memory back out tho,
