Carl J Lydick:
   => You're missing the fact that doing async I/O on most Unix
   => systems is simply impossible.  Sure, you could spawn a
   => subprocess for each I/O request you want to make, have each
   => subprocess issue a synchronous I/O request, then signal the
   => parent when it completed.  Seems like a fair amount of
   => additional overhead to do something that could be incorporated
   => in the system I/O routines.

Mark A Harrison:
   =Haven't you been paying attention?  Do we need to repost the man
   =pages?  These are modern times, we have THREADS these days!

Carl J Lydick:
   Some do.  A lot don't.

Hmm.. user level threads can be implemented on any system where
there's a user that has enough of an understanding of the underlying
machine to save machine state.  But, that's just an aside.

On those unix systems where "true asynch I/O" or "true [kernel]
threads" haven't been implemented, it's possible to achieve most of
the benefits of asynch i/o using select (synchronous multiplexed i/o)
in conjunction with non-blocking (polling) i/o.  You do have to do a
bit of queue management, but that's easy compared to issues like race
conditions [which don't go away when you go to kernel support for
asynch i/o].  But that's still an aside.

I've still not seen a discussion of the VMS library design where, in
essence, all VMS i/o is done with what would be an ioctl() under unix,
and there's all these useless arguments to be specified even in simple
cases where they're not needed.  Seems to me that VMS is providing a
low-level mechanism here, with all that implies.

Which brings me back to my judgement that Unix provides a lot more
("higher level") modularity than VMS.  Or, most of the benefits of VMS
have to do with it providing a lower level machine interface.
[Flashback to the "assembly" vs "high level language" wars of
yesteryear.]

