|> may also be a risk of the kernel getting stuck waiting to write to a hung TTY.
|> 
|> Hugh.

How about trying it on your shiny new Alpha?  It's there, but not really
documented ('man stty' kind of hints about the 'status' or 'info' function
but really doesn't tell you how to use it).

Issue the command: 

axposf.pa.dec.com> stty status ^t

then hit Ctrl-T a few times:

axposf.pa.dec.com> load: 1.46  cmd: csh 6163 [ttyin] 0.12u 0.12s 0% 248k
load: 1.46  cmd: csh 6163 [ttyin] 0.12u 0.12s 0% 248k
load: 1.46  cmd: csh 6163 [ttyin] 0.12u 0.12s 0% 248k
load: 1.46  cmd: csh 6163 [ttyin] 0.12u 0.12s 0% 248k
load: 1.46  cmd: csh 6163 [ttyin] 0.12u 0.12s 0% 248k

Let's check the output some:

axposf.pa.dec.com> uptime
07:51  up 11 days, 16:13,  19 users,  load average: 3.00, 3.00, 3.00

Well, the load average (1.46) doesn't correspond to anything uptime
has to report...

axposf.pa.dec.com> ps l
  UID   PID  PPID  CP PRI  NI   VSZ  RSS WCHAN  STAT TT       TIME COMMAND
10096  6163  6161   0  44   0 1.75M 424K -      R  + q3    0:00.25 -csh (csh)

axposf.pa.dec.com> ps u
USER       PID %CPU %MEM   VSZ  RSS TT  STAT STARTED       TIME COMMAND
cherkus   6163  0.0  0.2 1.75M 424K q3  R  + 07:38:07   0:00.26 -csh (csh)

The cmd name (csh) and pid (6163) is correct.  The event being waited
for (ttyin) is reasonable while the shell is waiting for my keystrokes.  
The ps output indicates csh was not waiting (i.e. was runnable) at the
time ps checked the process table, which is also reasonable.  The time
(0.12u + 0.12s = 0:00.24) is reasonable; the two ps commands indicate
0:00.25 and 0:00.26 which indicates that the shell did have to run .01
second to start ps.  The memory usages don't seem to agree (248k vs.
424K).

The two major things that don't agree (load average and memory usage)
could be because of a bug, or because they are calculating the usages
differently.

To show the command is useful, put some other job in the foreground
and hit Ctrl-T a few times as it runs:

axposf.pa.dec.com> find / -name \* -print  >& /dev/null
load: 1.56  cmd: find 6299 [event] 0.03u 0.25s 0% 160k
load: 1.56  cmd: find 6299 [running] 0.04u 0.26s 0% 160k
load: 1.56  cmd: find 6299 [running] 0.04u 0.26s 0% 160k
load: 1.54  cmd: find 6299 [event] 0.04u 0.27s 0% 160k
load: 1.54  cmd: find 6299 [event] 0.04u 0.29s 0% 160k
load: 1.53  cmd: find 6299 [event] 0.05u 0.38s 0% 160k
load: 1.53  cmd: find 6299 [event] 0.06u 0.40s 0% 160k
load: 1.53  cmd: find 6299 [event] 0.06u 0.43s 0% 160k
load: 1.51  cmd: find 6299 [event] 0.07u 0.46s 0% 160k
load: 1.51  cmd: find 6299 [event] 0.07u 0.49s 0% 160k
load: 1.51  cmd: find 6299 [event] 0.08u 0.54s 0% 160k
load: 1.50  cmd: find 6299 [event] 0.08u 0.58s 1% 160k
load: 1.50  cmd: find 6299 [event] 0.10u 0.71s 1% 160k
load: 1.59  cmd: find 6299 [event] 0.10u 0.75s 1% 160k
load: 1.59  cmd: find 6299 [event] 0.12u 0.87s 1% 160k
load: 1.57  cmd: find 6299 [event] 0.13u 0.94s 1% 160k

You can see that 'find' spends some time running and a fair amount of
time waiting for an event (i.e. disk I/O).  If you knew how long your
program took to run, this function could help you determine how much
longer it would take to finish.

I guess this post is appropriate for the vms-unix flame war because it
shows yet another nifty unix function yet it also shows an poorly
documented function that does not seem to provide information that is
entirely consistent with other system functions.  Oh, well, I guess
we gotta use up all of the internet's bandwidth somehow!

