Yet another clueless idiot make himself heard.  Each time a string is parsed,
quotes are stripped.  Thus, when DCL parses the assignment command:

	$ a="Paper: ""Very Interesting"" (really)"

The outer quotes are stripped, and the interior pairs of quotes are reduced to
one quote instead of two:
	$ a="Paper: "" Very Interesting"" (really)"
	$ WRITE SYS$OUTPUT a
	Paper: " Very Interesting" (really)
When you try using that in the command:
	$ mail/subj="''a'"
after the symbol a is substituted, we have:
	$ mail/subj="Paper: " Very Interesting" (really)"
which, as one would expect, uses Very as the name of the to be mailed.
On the other hand, if we use, as I suggested:
	$ a="Paper: """"Very Interesting"""" (really)"
	$ WRITE SYS$OUTPUT a
	Paper: ""Very Interesting"" (really)
then when we execute the command:
	$ mail/subj="''a'"
after symbol substitution we have:
	$ mail/subj="Paper: ""Very Interesting"" (really)"
which, when parsed, gives us as the subject
	Paper: "Very Interesting" (really)
Or, if we use, as Ehud suggested:
	$ a="""Paper: """"Very Interesting"""" (really) ""
	"Paper: ""Very Interesting"" (really) "
Then, after symbol substitution, we've once again got:
	$ mail/subj="Paper: ""Very Interesting"" (really) " file.txt recipient
which results in the subject:
	Paper: "Very Interesting" (really) 
Rather than waiting for O'Reilly and Associates to publish a book, you might
want to take a look at the DCL Concepts Manual.
