In article <ROCKWELL.94Mar11113811@nova.umd.edu>, rockwell@nova.umd.edu (Raul Deluth Miller) writes:
> Carl J Lydick:
>    Using the Unix approach to wild-carding ...  also means that you
>    can't give two wild-card arguments in a single command.  If you
>    want to do either of the above, you've got to pass the wildcard
>    itself, and not the expansion of the wildcard, to the program, or
>    have the program read the list of file names from a file instead of
>    taking the filespecs as an argument.
> 
> Or, have the shell do it for you:
> $ for x in *.[ch]; do cp $x ${x}_backup; done
> 
> Since unix allows you to use arbitrary commands in place of wild
> cards, you wind up with a lot more flexibility this way than you do
> with VMS's scheme.

Izzat so?  VMS does have a similar capability built into DCL:

	$	old_name = ""
	$ loop:	x = f$search (p1)
	$	if x .eqs. old_name .or. x .eqs. "" then exit
	$	old_name = x
	$	copy 'x' 'f$extract(0,f$locate(";",x),x)'_backup
	$	goto loop

Granted, the syntax is a little ugly;  it is notably less ugly if
you don't require appending the "_backup" string onto the file name
but instead want to copy the files into, eg, a backup directory.

I seriously doubt that there is anything very useful of this sort that
you can't do on both systems, after some fashion.  Each of them will
have some situations that are more awkward for that system than it is
for the other one (example:  it is often rather more awkward to arrange
a script in Unix that does wild-carding on non-file objects than it
usually is on VMS), but if you're willing to spend the time to figure
out how each of them works you should be able to find a way to accomplish
the task on both of them.

Bruce C. Wright


