Fred, 
 
For what it is worth, I suspect both you and Carl are correct.
 
The "Mother Tongue/First Love" argument is often correct, but not 
always. For example, while I started systems programming on IBM 
1620 Disk Monitor I, then IBM 1130 Disk Operating System, then 
OS/360 (HASP and ASP), I still find the RSX-11 family and its 
logical descendant (OpenVMS) more pleasant and consistent than 
the IBM products.
 
(Parenthetical comment: Those who complain about the size of the 
"Gray Wall" should look at the size of the Documentation for IBM 
mainframe products, where "Gray City" might be a more apt 
description)
 
However, there is an attraction to minimalist standards, it 
permits one to have the imagination to do it yourself (As Jerry 
Leichter noted earlier). Unfortunately, as Jerry noted, this 
leads to incompatible, parallel implementations.
 
I find it interesting to hear UNIX afficiados (sic) who claim the 
simplicity of the IO interface, and then attempt to explain the 
different parallel feature implementations in the C runtime 
library.
 
- Bob
