> |   Joe Crowe                                |
> |   Tandem Computers Inc.                    |
> |   Internet Address:jcrowe@mpd.tandem.com   | 
> ==============================================
-- 
Joe,
 
The point of asynchronous IO is not scatter gather, although, in 
a sense, you could use asynchronous IO to implement a scatter 
gather scheme. 
  
The point of asynchronous event is situations where you have 
multiple simultaneous activities proceeding. For example, 
consider a process which has an outstanding terminal IO, a 
network link to manage, and a real-time device to control. The 
sequence of event completions (and possible exceptions) on the 
different devices is unpredictable. On OpenVMS, the best way to 
implement this is through the $QIO system service, using AST 
routines to receive control when each particular operation 
complete (alternatively, one could use DECthreads to implement 
the same functionality). 
 
If you used blocking IO, then you would not notice anything wrond 
with your network link until the realtime IO or terminal 
operation completed. Programs which use synchronous (blocking) IO 
in such situations tend to fall victim to the "Printer out of 
paper" catastrophe, where the entire system comes to a screaching 
halt because the console printer ran out of paper.
 
I hope that this explanation has been helpful. If I have been 
unclear, or if I can be of further assistance, please feel free 
to contact me via Email or phone.
 
- Bob
