In article <2ltlsq$cgc@gap.cco.caltech.edu>, carl@SOL1.GPS.CALTECH.EDU 
(Carl J Lydick) writes...

>VAX-11 and MicroVAX are the two previous architectures.  VAX-11 architecture
>machines are no longer sold by DEC.

Sorry, Carl, but this just isn't so.  VAX-11 and MicroVAX were never considered
separate architectures.  There has been only one VAX architecture; it has
various subsets of instructions which may be omitted, but there was never
anything called "MicroVAX architecture".

It is true that current VAX models implement a subset that is close to
that originally implemented by the MicroVAX II, but that doesn't make
them any less "VAX architecture".

"DEC Standard 032" is the VAX Architecture; it's essentially what you can
buy as the VAX Architecture Reference Manual (plus some internal-only
implementation notes).  There was never any separate standard defined
for a "MicroVAX architecture".  Indeed, not all MicroVAX systems implemented
the same set.

I was the VMS developer who integrated floating point instruction emulation
into VMS back when the MicroVAX I was released.  I recall writing a letter
to Datamation explaning that all VAX software ran on the MicroVAX I (they
had earlier published a letter from a competitor claiming that there
was no software for this new machine.)  I've been heavily involved in
VAX architecture issues as far back as 1979 when we added G and H float
(and I implemented the RTL support for them.)  I believe I can speak
with authority on this subject.

