Bryan J Jensen (bjj@ECLD.PSU.EDU) wrote:

> Then I found I had to create some processes.  UNIX requires the
> parent process to do a "wait()" on the child process before it will
> terminate a child.  Since I can't wait around for processes to
> finish, I need some mechanism to let me know when a process
> terminates so that I can safely do the wait to complete its rundown.

Try a SIGCHLD... details are in just about any unix programming
book, if you can't find it in the man page.  Here's a handy
tip:  grep can be used as a nice indexing tool in the man
page directories.  (flame protection -- It's also in the SUNOS master
index, under both "process" and "termination").  Here's the
relevant information:

    If the parent process of the calling process is executing  a
    wait() or waitpid(), or is interested in the SIGCHLD signal,
    then it is notified of the calling process's termination and
    the  low-order eight bits of status are made available to it
    (see wait(2V)).

> Unfortunately, "select" may only be used to wait for certain types of
> I/O to complete - it can't be used to wait for child process termination.
> "wait" can be used to wait for any process to complete, but it can't
> be used to wait for I/O to complete.

That's because I/O and processes are two different things.

> UNIX, on the other hand, patched async facilities in later, which seems
> to be in keeping with the general haphazard nature of UNIX (that's
> not really a criticism - diversity is politically correct these days).

That's one of the reasons Unix has succeeded against all odds over
the past 25 years.  It adapts.  (TCP/IP has had the same characteristics.)

> I think the one thing that hurts most under VMS is that all system calls
> return a status code.  As a result, programmers feel obligated to test
> for errors.  Contrast that with UNIX where errors are rarely checked,
> with the resulting frequent core dumps on null pointers.  So it's
> really just a tradeoff between developement time and reliability.

This is a bit of an ad hominem:  VMS returns error codes and VMS
programmers feel obligated to check these codes, Unix returns
error codes and Unix programmers rarely check error codes.

Disclaimer:  I'm so picky that I even check the return code
from printf().

