Yes, both of these are true in many cases.

> I do a large amount of work with VMS, and many other systems and 
> packages. I would have to say that no shop is truly portable 
> until they have placed ALL of their applications on at least 3 
> platforms. Invariably, the problems caused by incompatibilities 
> and extensions cause major problems.

I agree with all of this.  My experience is based on porting
commercial software packages to various hardware platforms.
It was frustrating to deal with gratuitous incompatibilities
among different Unix boxes, but there was nowhere near the
effort involved in making the VMS port.

That's why I feel that VMS (and other operating systems that operate
only on one manufacturer's platforms) are in for some hard times ahead.
It's just less and less economically feasible to support them.

For example, suppose I write an application.  I'm a decent "portable"
Unix programmer, so it's not to hard to port it to DEC equipment
running OSF/1, in addition to HP, IBM, and Sun.  Then, I port it to
Windows/NT (let's assume that NT becomes the popular successor
to windows).  Now I've got a big base of Intel boxes, AND DEC equipment
running NT.  There's just not the cost justification in terms of
porting and support to move to VMS.

This kind of thing feeds on itself.  DEC customers see more and more
"new" stuff for NT and OSF/1, and start viewing VMS as as "legacy"
system.  That doesn't mean that VMS is dead now, or even ten years
from now.  It's just that it's on the "back end" of it's (very
successful) life-cycle curve.

> Remember, while it is true 
> that you can only buy VMS hardware manufactured by DIGITAL 
> (directly or indirectly), the same is true of SPARC stations, 
> RS/6000, IRIX, etc.

Actually, not true of Sparc boxes.  Sun has release Sparc to
a consortium, which then sublicences to other manufacturers
(I think it costs only $100 to join).  I know of at least two
Asian Sparc workstation clone manufacturers -- Hyundai and Tatung.
There are also some people specializing in Sparc embedded systems.

Very soon, I think we're going to see the same thing happen
with the Power PC architecture.

