In article <1994Mar12.184813.513@rlgsc.com>,  <gezelter@rlgsc.com> wrote:
...
>The point of asynchronous event is situations where you have 
>multiple simultaneous activities proceeding. For example, 
>consider a process which has an outstanding terminal IO, a 
>network link to manage, and a real-time device to control. The 
>sequence of event completions (and possible exceptions) on the 
>different devices is unpredictable. On OpenVMS, the best way to 
>implement this is through the $QIO system service, using AST 
>routines to receive control when each particular operation 
>complete (alternatively, one could use DECthreads to implement 
>the same functionality). 

I haven't really been following this whole flame fest, but the new topic
caught my attention.  Forgive me if I misunderstand the capabilitiy being
requested here.  POSIX does provide non-blocking I/O.  You can set a file
to be non-blocking using fcntl with an O_NONBLOCK parameter.  You can also
set it to send your process a SIGIO signal when I/O completes.  You can
also use select() with any file to determine whether a read or write would
block.  The only thing which is tough to implement is the equivalent of
a read completion AST.  However, on systems which support POSIX threads,
the effect of a read completion AST can be achieved much more easily by
using blocking I/O in another thread.  On OSF/1, this approach is very
efficient, while on some other platforms, it is usually actually implemented
using select().

