I did get _one_ benefit out of this one.  I have come to understand *why*
UN*X apps are so persistently hostile to touch typists.  To recap:  changing
UN*X tty mode between raw and cooked causes stdin to be flushed, thanks to 
some Berzerkeleyite whom I daily wish I could have (a) shot, (b) drawn and
quartered, (c) tarred and feathered, (d) run out of town on a rail, and 
finally (e) buried in a cesspool.  This behavior is beyond my ability to 
fix, since it is *not* user-level code.  Applications may attempt to make
their behavior more friendly to touch typists (although almost none of them
do) by first buffering in any extant stdin, then changing tty mode.  However,
such applications *still* cannot _guarantee_ friendliness to touch typists,
because they cannot guarantee avoidance of race conditions (consider a *fast*
typist on a system that has been fork-bombed--and some of the systems I have
to work on (EPA's National Environmental Supercomputing Center machines are
examples) do a good imitation).

What a UN*X applications program would need to be able to do in order to 
guarantee friendliness to touch typists is something like the following:

    establish critical section, locking stdin
    buffer existing contents of stdin
    change tty mode
    release critical section
    process previously buffered contents of stdin
    process stdin

Of course, since UN*X has no facilities for establishing such critical
sections, applications programs have no way to guarantee that race conditions
cannot arise between me at my keyboard and the app, buffering stdin and changing 
tty mode.  Most UN*X apps don't even _try_.  I understand from other 
conversations elsewhere that the situation on certain blue-colored 
mainframes is even worse... glad I don't have to deal with _that_!


