We are considering, as one of our tape backup options, purchasing 
TLZ06-VA 4mm DAT tape drives for our new VAX systems (4100, 31xx, VS40x0) 
which are using StorageWorks BA353 "pizza boxes" for the drives.  We have used 
8mm systems in the past with a lot of success, but the 4mm format, especially 
with the fact that you can just slide one of these into a 3.5" StorageWorks 
slot, and the lower cost, is appealing.

Our dilemma is that the Novell bunch in our company have been using 4mm DAT 
for some time, and have had a *lot* of problems with them, so much so that 
they are switching to 8mm.  Typical problems: a tape written in one drive 
can't be read in another *identical* drive on another identical system.  5 or 
6 burned out drive motors on various drives in 1 year.  Lots of failures. 
 Basically, they say 4mm is just not robust.

Please email or post any experience, good or bad, with, specifically, DEC DAT 
drives (we are only considering DEC TLZ06).  Are they reliable and robust?  Is 
the whole 4mm standard less robust than 8mm, as our Novell group would be 
inclined to think, or did they pick a bad supplier (I don't know who they 
were)?

Hopefully this post will not be drowned in the "Is VMS dead" flame wars :-)

