In article <2lve53$qpt@gap.cco.caltech.edu>,
Carl J Lydick <carl@SOL1.GPS.CALTECH.EDU> wrote:
>In article <2lutef$47t@news.utdallas.edu>, mharriso@utdallas.edu (Mark A Harrison) writes:
>=Carl J Lydick (carl@SOL1.GPS.CALTECH.EDU) wrote:
>=> VAX-11 and MicroVAX are the two previous architectures.  VAX-11 architecture
>=> machines are no longer sold by DEC.
>=
>=Are you sure about this?  I was under the impression that architecturally
>=these were parts of the same family, like Sun has with the Sparc.  Some
>=CPU's may be faster or have more features, but there was a baseline
>=architecture that they all adhered to.  That's why you could have
>=binary compatibility across a wide array of price and performance.
>
>Though I've always, in the past, heard them referred to as separate
>architectures, someone from DEC has posted that they're considered to be the
>same architecture.  As to "binary compatibility," a good part of that was
>achieved by generating an exception on the non-VAX-11 machines when one of the
>instructions not supported on later VAXen was seen, and calling library
>routines to emulate the instructions.  If I recall correctly, there are
>packages out there that allow you to run MS-DOS executables on a VAX.  Does
>that make the VAX and the PC the same architecture?

Before the stones come flying my way and I have to duck, let me prefix
this by saying I have done and am still doing development under both VMS
and Unix ...

Okay.  So we're up to three architectures that *today* can run VMS, two
of which I can currently buy from DEC.  One cannot infer that VMS is
available on more than those three architectures as the orginal
statement implied, however the ability for VMS to be ported to more
than those three architectures may be certainly be possible. Unix, on
the other hand, currently does run on more than three architectures.
Yes, the architecture-dependant portion of the kernel must be written,
but the same would hold true for any OS that runs on a variety of
architectures.

I've sat back for a while now and watched this thread argue the merits of
VMS vs. Unix.  It is important that one pick the right tool for the job.
It is possible to write portable code that will run under both VMS
and Unix, but you may have to give up some of the important merits of
your particular OS.

If you write code native to VMS, that code will outperform the same code
written in a language such as C to be portable to a variety of operating
systems.  The same holds true for Unix in process creation, for instance.
I have often modified my designs to accomodate both operating systems
simply due to the fact that one operating system can do something the
other one can't.  When modifying the design would result in a severe
performace degredation on one of the operating systems, I would opt in
favor of doing things in the native method of the operating system.
