In article <1994Mar14.183954.21481@integrity.uucp>, jcrowe@mpd.tandem.com (Joseph Crowe) writes:
> In article <1994Mar11.222806.18797@avatar.montagar.com>, davidc@avatar.montagar.com (David L. Cathey) writes:
> |> 	Case history?  Data analysis of 50+ data files across multiple spindles
> |> (about 750MBytes).  Analysis took over 7 hours with normal I/O.  Asynch the I/O
> |> across several spindles at once (Using RMS no less) and the elapsed time was
> |> reduced to about 25 minutes.  If you can't see that as a benefit, then you're
> |> still missing something.
> 
>     As of an earlier reply, I admit seeing the uses of async i/o....but in your example
> above, you reduce the impact of i/o waits for the individual application but at
> what impact to the timesharing nature of the machine?

	Well, the new application would pretty much suck a CPU clean of any
idle CPU cycles - this was considered a good thing.  Impact on the normal
time-sharing environment could be regulated through use of process priority,
QUANTUM, additional CPU's in a multi-CPU system, and now with Class Scheduling
in V6.0 (V6.1 on AXP this summer).

>                                                        When the first realtime
> variants of UNIX came to market, I began to wonder whether it made sense to
> use a "general purpose" OS for a particular paradigm.

	If the "general purpose" OS has the required features, and unneeded
components that would negatively effect processing can be turned off, I see
no reason why not.  On VAXes, there is the option of using ELN, a real-time
"VMS"-like system that has a lot of the time-sharing features stripped out.
XLN is available (or will soon be???) for the AXP.

	All things considered, DOS is a real-time OS.  A program pretty much
has complete control of the CPU.  If you need time-sharing, it makes a lousy
choice, but for a real-time application it's fine since anything else in the
OS would just be overhead (Windows, OS/2, Linux).

>                                                        This is just a throw it
> out for general discussion point.  I'm not placing any relative value on UNIX,
> VMS or any other OS but let's kick off a new direction.  For a general purpose
> multi-processor system, when you support realtime type of applications at the
> highest priority, does the timesharing application have to suffer?  I believe it
> does.

	In true real-time, YES, time-sharing does suffer.  But that's the
nature of the beast.  The reason the application is real-time is because it
is very time critical.  Waiting for a user to give up the CPU could cause
lost data or corruption (very undesirable).  Multi-processor systems (on VMS,
anyway) could make real-time applications have an "affinity" for a select CPU
or set of CPU's to reduce impact on time-share applications, but in general
the users have to put up with erratic performance,

