In article <2lv1qi$47t@news.utdallas.edu>, mharriso@utdallas.edu (Mark A Harrison) writes:
> Chris Kalisiak (kalisiak@cs.buffalo.edu) wrote:
>> Ok, IBM with AIX. HP with HP-UX. Sun with Solaris. DEC with OSF/1. 
>> SGI with IRIX. Am I leaving anyone important out? They are all proprietary
>> and are all Unix.
> 
> Yawn... you're missing the point.  Let me know what word you want to
> use for operating systems written by the hardware vendor for a
> specific piece of their own hardware and I'll rephrase the question
> in a way you'll be certain to understand.  However, I think that
> you really do understand the point.

	No, he really isn't missing the point.  Each vendors Unix _is_
proprietary.  Each vendors Unix has _different_ features, though there is
quite a bit of commonality.  

	For example:  We have had two different descriptions of asynch I/O
for two different Unix systems.  Porting an application using these functions
between Unix systems would not be easy, and depending upon the individual
Unix, possibly extremely difficult.

	If you *really* want to program in a portable language, go program in
COBOL, Pascal, or FORTRAN.  These languages provide their own interface which
can be adapted to the OS, whereas C defines the expected interface to the OS.
 
