In article <ROCKWELL.94Mar7232940@nova.umd.edu>,
Raul Deluth Miller <rockwell@nova.umd.edu> wrote:
>>Carl J Lydick:
>>	   % grep * file.name
>
>* isn't a valid search string for grep, even if it were quoted.  * is
>a meta character such that x* represents 0 or more occurences of x.  x
>may be a single character, a set of characters, a string, a set of
>strings, or some other arbitrary regular expression.
>
>As far as I know,    grep '*' files    will just search for a literal 
> asterisk, but even that is rather bad form.  

In the context of the thread leading to Carl's example, he was showing
that the shell may (i tested it with csh) perform helpful things like:
 Expand the wildcard to a space-separated list of your local filenames...
 then hand -that- to grep as the string to locate in every other file
  (in my test, a file named "8mm" became the string searched for in all
    other files...) (i assume it'll get around to file.name someday).
A consequence of the shell blindly expanding wildcards to space-separated
 filenames, without knowing what the application -really- wanted.  In VMS
 the application would ask the shell (DCL) for the "search_string", and
 then later for the "filename".  DCL, using a user (or DEC-) supplied
 parsing table, would unambiguosly hand back the parts of the command line
 (along with flags indicating if there were comma-separated multiple parts
 to -that- portion of the argument list).

In an attempt to see what SEARCH thought of "search file.name *", and
 other attempts to confuse it... the above ^^^ located asterisks in the
 named file.  It also found "%" and "." (without quoting them).
A comma confused DCL: and it confessed to that.  DCL tried to use the
 comma as a separator between multiple filenames... and decided that there
 was no additional name beyond the comma.  The result was that the application
 was -not- called, and DCL reported:
   %DCL-W-NULFIL, missing or invalid file specification - respecify

(just for chuckles: HELP ERROR told me to use the form HELP/MESSAGE NULFIL,
 the text of which stated that DCL expected a file specification to follow
 a comma.  So VMS told me in english what it -thought- was going on, which
 (hopefully) would allow me to adjust what i was trying to tell it.
  (HELP SEARCH, of course, actually said that i should surround 
    non-alphanumerics in quotes (" "), but who reads HELP first?))

--dick


