|> NAME
|>      aioread, aiowrite - asynchronous I/O operations

[man page deleted for brevity]

|> While I agree that the some people may be confusing the normal behaviour of
|> Unix write (where the data is not actually transferred to disk before the call
|> returns) with real asynchronous I/O as supported by some of the VMS system
|> services, it's not true to say that you can't do asynchronous I/O under any
|> version of Unix.  The above stuff was apparently added in SunOS 4.1 - other
|> versions of Unix may or may not include the same facility.

   I agree that most UNIX versions at this point do not provide async i/o
capabilities at the user level.  My question is....so what?  Why should the
user concern h(er|im)self with the intricacies of scatter/gather i/o at
the API level when it automatically gets handled in a hopefully optimal
way on h(er|is) behalf by the kernel and device drivers.  Or am I missing
something.  I remember doing scatter/gather async i/o almost twenty
years ago on the then UNIVAC 1100 mainframe.  It's a nice exercise
to demonstrate what goes on at low levels.  I believe that with the 
addition of thread support in SVR4 ES/MP we will probably see 
async i/o at the user level.  I think it would make more sense in a
multiprocessor system.  If I'm missing something here, I fell sure one
of the reader/contributor types in this newsgroup will let me know.
But please, the use of words like idiot and moron in lieu of calm,
reasoned debate will be piped to the blackhole device.
