Mark,
 
Vendor independence is nice, but, alas, few sites are truly 
vendor independent.
 
As examples, consider the following common statements:
 
   "We use UNIX because of portability. We find the PERFORMER 
    library extremely useful" (NOTE: PERFORMER is a library 
    running on SILICON GRAPHICS' workstations running IRIX.
    SG has no intention of porting PERFORMER to any other 
    platforms. The underlying GL technology, however, will 
    be portable through implementations of the OpenGL standard.
 
    "We run on 200+ different types of systems. IBM, HP, AST, 
    COMPAQ, etc." Nice portability, as long as you are running
    MS-DOS.
 
Without being long-winded, let me say that many people complain 
endlessy about closed vendor shops, and then go on to create on.
 
I do a large amount of work with VMS, and many other systems and 
packages. I would have to say that no shop is truly portable 
until they have placed ALL of their applications on at least 3 
platforms. Invariably, the problems caused by incompatibilities 
and extensions cause major problems. Remember, while it is true 
that you can only buy VMS hardware manufactured by DIGITAL 
(directly or indirectly), the same is true of SPARC stations, 
RS/6000, IRIX, etc.
 
