In article <1994Mar11.190110.20289@integrity.uucp>, jcrowe@zaphodmpd.tandem.com (Joseph Crowe) writes:
>    I agree that most UNIX versions at this point do not provide async i/o
> capabilities at the user level.  My question is....so what?  Why should the
> user concern h(er|im)self with the intricacies of scatter/gather i/o at
> the API level when it automatically gets handled in a hopefully optimal
> way on h(er|is) behalf by the kernel and device drivers.  Or am I missing
> something.

	You're missing something.  I/O is the slowest thing you can do.
The advantage of timesharing systems give the O/S something to do (someone
elses job) while your in a wait state for an I/O to complete.  In several
cases, there are many things your job could be doing rather than wait on
an I/O.  It's another nethod to avoid wasting cycles.  With the speed of
processors across the entire computing spectrum increasing, I/O waits are
taking larger chunks of your elapsed time.  And its irritating to managers
to buy a faster machine that hardly makes a dent in elapsed time.

	Asynch I/O is how to optimize your use of CPU vs. I/O.  True there
is some help in the caching, buffering, but you are still stalled at
various points because an I/O request isn't done (and your return status is
really accurately reflecting reality).  Asynch I/O is comparable to parallel
processing, and like parallel processing, the O/S needs help in determining
which operations are not truly idempotent or can be interleaved.  Asynch
operations can be absolutely invaluable in client-server applications where
several transactions are going on concurrently.

	Case history?  Data analysis of 50+ data files across multiple spindles
(about 750MBytes).  Analysis took over 7 hours with normal I/O.  Asynch the I/O
across several spindles at once (Using RMS no less) and the elapsed time was
reduced to about 25 minutes.  If you can't see that as a benefit, then you're
still missing something.

