> .  have all the file names at the very end of the command line?  
> 
> Huh?  What's your restriction here?

	COPY *.C *.C_BACKUP

	Where does the end of the first argument expansion end and first
begin?  If there is no current *.C_BACKUP the command fails, but if there is
the results are completely different but incorrect. There is no hint given
by the shell as to where the two arguments ar.

> .  As to what purpose, that's the programmers decision, and shouldn't
> .  be forced because of the nature of what the shell is going to do.
> 
> If you don't like what the shell is going to do, you don't have to use
> the shell.

	But for a general purpose program, someone else may use the shell.
Or a different shell.  If I'm not going to use the shell to call my program,
what should I use?

> .  For example:
> .	   $ SEARCH *.* STRING
> .  I decide I like a "grep" like organization, so I change the command
> .  definition (but no code) to get:
> .	   $ GREP STRING *.*
> 
> What is it about the command definition that makes it "not code"?  I
> had more trouble finding doc on command definitions than I had finding
> doc on using the linker.  And, command definitions have a lot of
> complexity to them.  Seems to me that command definitions are just a
> specialized programming language.

	In a way you're correct.  Consider it an interface between the shell
and the program.  The command definition provides the shellhoww the command
should look to the user, then provides the information to the program
that it wants.  It separates the syntax of the command from the program.
It add modularity to the DCL/command-syntax/program interfaces.

	X-window widgets work in a similar fashion, instead of gadgets and
widgets, you have parameters and qualifiers that trigger different sections
of code.

	I'm not sure what problems you've had with the linker, but 99% of 
the time a "LINK myprog" works just fine...  Or "LINK myprog,rnt1,rnt2,..."

