In article <2lnhda$kc@charm.magnus.acs.ohio-state.edu> JONESD@kcgl1.eng.ohio-state.edu (David Jones) writes:
...
>>I can do asynchronous I/O under Unix.
>
>I strongly doubt it.  You can fork a separate process to perform the
>I/O or do Non-blocking I/O but in neither case are you really doing
>asynchronous I/O....

There are several brands of unix that support true asynchronous io.
For example, ibm's AIX uses a separate asynchronous device, /dev/aio0
and calls specific to it.  These calls include:

aio_cancel	Cancels one or more asynchronous I/O requests.
aio_error	Retrieves the error status of an I/O request.
aio_read	Reads asynchronously from a file.
aio_return	Retrieves the return value of an I/O request.
aio_write	Writes asynchronously to a file.
aio_suspend	Blocks until an asynchronous I/O is completed.
lio_listio	Initiates multiple asynchronous read and write operations.

You are certainly correct that asynchronous io was not present in older
versions of unix, but that is certainly not the case now.

