In article <2ln469$jdh@zebedee.ingres.co.uk>, timh@ingres.co.uk (Tim
Holmes) writes:
>David L. Cathey (davidc@avatar.montagar.com) wrote:
>:     So, you can use RMS to deal with a file as a series of record (just
>: like using stdio or other set of library routines), use RMS or the XQP
to
>: access the file as a series of bytes,  or skip all that and do direct
I/O
>: to the device.
>
>RMS is Record Management Services. If you don't need to work at this
level you
>don't have to. RMS is merely a set of routines for working with data at
>record level. I believe most Unix followers could live with
>that - however RMS is also the standard interface, access via XQP or QIO
is
>not available to your average COBOL programmer unless a systems programmer
>is around to provide an interface. In fact to get the best out of RMS this
>latter approach is essential (especially for lock handling).
>

This is true of COBOL period -- (VMS, UNIX or whatever).  COBOL only
provides record level access to files (usingthe COBOL verbs).  This is
part of the language spec.  On VMS, RMS is used to do this. On Unix,
runtime routines are provided by the language.  All this means is that the
same file may be processed by a FORTRAN program on VMS, but on UNIX this
would depend on the two compilers using the same data format for breaking
the files into records (FORTRAN is also record oriented).





