In article <ROCKWELL.94Mar8170842@nova.umd.edu>, rockwell@nova.umd.edu (Raul Deluth Miller) writes:
> Chris Lishka:
> .  On the other hand, Unix presents tape drives as a device "file",
> .  and doesn't bother trying to extend the filesystem view to a tape.
> .  As far as Unix is concerned, there is >no< filesystem on a tape.
> .  One has to use tape-oriented commands to determine the structure on
> .  a tape, whereas the Unix filesystem is infused in the OS (and in
> .  the Unix "philosophy").
> 
> Eh?  You can also treat a disk as simply a device file.  Treating a
> device file as a file system requires (a) preparing an empty file
> system, then (b) mounting the file system.  Seems to me this should
> work with tapes as well as with disks -- be a fair bit slower, of
> course.

	I've seen Unix mount block-addressable tapes as file systems, too.
More than a "fair bit" slower...  So the contention that there is NO
filesystem on a tape is false.  FYI: It was a DECTAPE I drive on a PDP-11/70,
Unix Version 7.

> Of course, it could be argued that file system code doesn't belong in
> the kernel...

	Not a bad point.  On VMS, the file system is implemented by cooperation
between the actual processes.  Except for the actual disk I/O, all file
system knowledge is held by the processes.  Previously, the file system was
handled by ACP processes that actually understood the ODS-2 structure.

	The file system code is located in system space, and the data
structures (especially the I/O database structures) are located in system
space, and the code is executed in kernel mode, but the code is executed
as a process context thread.

