Overcome by nausea on 2 Mar 1994 21:50:25 GMT, Carl J Lydick
(carl@SOL1.GPS.CALTECH.EDU) retched up these words:

: Been out of touch for a while, eh?  You can now get the full doc set on CD-ROM.
: And you seem to be forgetting that, in part because the man pages WERE intended
: to be on disk in most systems, they're much less thorough than the VMS doc set.
: Unix offered you one level of help:  Large enough to tie up lots of disk space;
: small enough to very often be less than helpful.  VMS offered two:  One on-line
: that doesn't take up a whole lot of disk space, but wasn't complete; one in
: printed manuals that was much more thorough than Unix man pages.

As I said, these were my impressions from having used VMS 4 or 5 years
ago.  Of course, today Unix has the CD-ROM on-line documentation too,
but the CD-ROM products tend to vary from one manufacturer to another so
it's good to know the standard man pages are still around.

The Unix design philosophy has always been `full speed ahead, the
hardware will catch up.'  I can remember administering a HP-UX system
with a total of maybe 120 MB of disk space used for software development
by about 4 people.  We always deleted the man pages; having them on line
was just a luxury we couldn't afford with so little disk space.  Back
then you might have argued that the VMS way was better, but what sense
does it make today when 1 GB drives are going for <$800?  

Another consideration worth mentioning here: the excessive size of VMS'
documentation is caused by the excessive size of VMS.  VMS is cluttered
with features and needless complexity.  How many parameters does QIO
have, 12? 14?  There is no arbitrary limit on the size of a Unix man
page and some of them are quite huge.  Take a look at the man page for
csh or curses.  It's not that the man pages skimp on details, it's just
that in Unix' clean elegant design fewer commands, system calls, library
routines, parameters, etc. are needed to accomplish a task than would be
the case in VMS.

