I've system managed both systems.
 
The commands and libraries are basically the same.
 
The behavior underneath feels quite a bit more like unix than AIX.
 
According to the trade press, AIX is going to be a personality on
a new kernel base sometime in the future, I don't know what effect
this will have on performance.
 
AIX was never designed to do MP, so scalability options are an issue.
DEC OSF/1 was always intended to be SMP, and there are clear plans for
binary compatible scalability through SMP and i unicx clustering.
 
Storage mgmt is nice, using AdvFS, LSM, and storageworx hot-
pluggable SCSI components.
 
LSM does mirroring and striping, and has a neat GUI that let's you
make storage infrastructure changes on-line, no down time to
application users.
 
The Advabnced file system + utilities gives you on-line file system
expansion and contraction, on-line backups, on line file defragmentation
on-line striping of "hot" files for live performance tuning, etc.
 
I'd take OSF on Alpha over AIX for these reasons and more, including
performance and price performance with a clear non-disruptive path
to the future.
 
Please, let's handle th e rest off-linre, as I sound too much like a sales
rep.
