David L. Cathey (davidc@avatar.montagar.com) wrote:
: 	So, you can use RMS to deal with a file as a series of record (just
: like using stdio or other set of library routines), use RMS or the XQP to
: access the file as a series of bytes,  or skip all that and do direct I/O
: to the device.

RMS is Record Management Services. If you don't need to work at this level you
don't have to. RMS is merely a set of routines for working with data at 
record level. I believe most Unix followers could live with
that - however RMS is also the standard interface, access via XQP or QIO is
not available to your average COBOL programmer unless a systems programmer
is around to provide an interface. In fact to get the best out of RMS this
latter approach is essential (especially for lock handling).

The problem then is that RMS is great and provides a useful, common interface
to data stored in this way. There use to be a time when RMS was treated as a
performance obstacle, but this is now insignificant when compared to relational
databases such as Oracle/Ingres (which both bypass RMS altogether). 

What all these have in common is that they provide a consistent interface to 
data, moving the emphasis away from just data towards more abstract terms 
such as knowledge and information. I have this gut feeling that much of RMS is
now obsolete, symptomatic of a pre-relational age. Sure a system based around 
RMS would outperform a relational system, in the same way a macro program could
run faster than a Basic equivalent. This I believe is part of the rationale
behind the bundling of Rdb into VMS.

RMS is part of VMS, but VMS is much more. Its a production system, tried and
tested and if I were pulling the purse strings thats were I'd invest my dosh.
Especially when it comes to running mission critical systems such as those 
involved in banking applications. 

On the other side of the coin I recognize that Unix is equally as strong but
in different areas (this bloody vi isn't one of them). Workstation technology
_is_ one of them. Process creation to be exact. It has the added advantage that 
I can even afford to run it (Linux) at home. 

IMHO much of this flame war crap is based upon fear of the alternative. 
Be smart, get a foot in both camps - even better grow another and keep 
it in microsofts just in case...

