Carl, you win the Mike Dahmus (a good offense is the best defense) award
for 1994. :-)

Unix and VMS are very different operating systems and neither can cover
all the bases. I don't see why they can't coexist.

While I admire many of the technical features of VMS, I just can't see
people using VMS for fun. VMS is a working full-featured system, while
Unix is a do-it-yourself effort. I think the fact that Unix "doesn't
quite work" is one of the main things that draws people to it.

A lot of sysadmins enjoy crafting a set of utilities and compiling
free software that makes Unix "usable". With VMS you get a usable
system with very little left to do.

So I think there are probably many people out there who think VMS is
a better operating system, but who prefer Unix. And why not? There's
no real need for a battle over it. Just go on using the system you
prefer... If it dies (and OS's _do_ die), don't brood but learn
something new. In general your favorite O.S. is the one you know best.

- Travis

