In article <ROCKWELL.94Mar10070553@nova.umd.edu>, rockwell@nova.umd.edu (Raul Deluth Miller) writes:
>Carl J Lydick:
>.  Unix doesn't have support asynchronous I/O...
>
>Huh?  What do you mean?
>
>I can do asynchronous I/O under Unix.

I strongly doubt it.  You can fork a separate process to perform the
I/O or do Non-blocking I/O but in neither case are you really doing
asynchronous I/O.

In VMS, asynchronous I/O means that an I/O request that is not completed
immediately is queued (left pending) until the driver can complete the
request (read,write,etc).  In Unix, non-blocking I/O is still synchronous,
your request always completes (sometimes with EWOULDBLOCK) before the process
continues.  The select() call then provides a hint that your program can
probably re-try the operation with success.


