Anybody who uses ANSI labeled tapes.  It's a very simple file structure:  A
series of files on the tape.  But it's still a file structure.  Each file on
the tape is identified by the ANSI labels surrounding the data part of the
file.

=In the nine years that I have used Unix, I have never had a need to
=put a filesystem on a tape or use a tape based filesystem.  I still
=stand by my original statement: I >like< the fact that Unix (out of
=the box, no extra bits added) seperates the functionality of tapes and
=disks, even if it is only a "de facto" standard.

As opposed the the actual ANSI standard.

=I see no need to
=force tapes into a disk-like filesystem view (my personal preference).

And VMS does NOT do that.  It allows you to either deal with the tapes as a
tape-like (not a disk-like) filesystem (ANSI standard) or as a raw tape. 
Again, you're bitching that the fact that VMS gives extra functionality, and
allows you to write tapes conformant with a standard is somehow a deficiency in
VMS, and not in Unix which has no support for the standatd.
