=.  > What are you asking here?  How does "arranging the arguments
=.  > differently" accomplish any purpose?
=
=	   What I'm asking is what if you are writing an application,
=.  what if you want to arrange the arguments differents rather than
=.  have all the file names at the very end of the command line?  
=
=Huh?  What's your restriction here?

Using the Unix approach to wild-carding results in a variable number of
arguments being passed to the program.  That means that, using that approach,
any wild-card argument must be the last argument given in the command line
(unless, of course, you require that any options argument have a character
that's illegal in a file name to distinguigh it from a possible filename).  It
also means that you can't give two wild-card arguments in a single command.  If
you want to do either of the above, you've got to pass the wildcard itself, and
not the expansion of the wildcard, to the program, or have the program read the
list of file names from a file instead of taking the filespecs as an argument.
