Carl J Lydick:
.  Fine.  If you want the ported file to have an indexed organization,
.  then write your own software to support that sort of file
.  organization (because Unix doesn't have any standard software to do
.  it), and issue the following commands on your VMS system:
	   $ CONVERT/FDL=SYS$INPUT: old_file new_file
	   RECORD
		   FORM	STREAM_LF
.  You've now got a file that's "just a stream of bytes."  You can do
.  with it whatever you want on the Unix system.

Why bother converting from VMS in the first place?  If you're happy
with VMS, there's no reason.  If you're happy with Unix, there's no
reason.

If you've a hetrogenous system, you'll probably modularize on
functional lines.  There doesn't seem to be any particular advantage
in moving VMS data verbatim onto a Unix system.  If there was, why
bother with the extra VMS functionality in the first place?

Or, from the Unix side of things: why bother introducing extra VMS
complexities into the system?  Why not just make the VMS machine a
print server so it can do something useful?

Raul D. Miller
<rockwell@nova.umd.edu>


