David L. Cathey:
.	   I was not referencing "man" in particular, but try to
.  explain to a DOS user about awk, grep, biff, sed, perl, ...

awk is a bit like basic.
grep is a bit like find.
biff is a bit like castoff/caston.
sed is vaguely similar to edlin.
perl is sort of like an object-oriented basic+.

If I were teaching a dos user these things, I'd probably teach the
following syntax:
  awk -fprogram
  fgrep 'string' files
  biff n/biff y
  use ed for now.
  perl program

Also, I'd probably say -- if you have perl, don't bother with awk.
Finally, with perl, I'd probably make up a language summary of how to
do a few basic-like things and say to call me if they need more
information.  [I'd also point out where to get real documentation, but
a little hand holding at the beginning never hurt no one].

Raul D. Miller
<rockwell@nova.umd.edu>


