In <2lg4pe$419@gap.cco.caltech.edu> carl@SOL1.GPS.CALTECH.EDU (Carl J Lydick) writes:

>It appears to me that the ability to load a stream of bytes into memory and
>transfer control to that stream of bytes is THE atomic form of an operating
>system.  All other functions can be imposed/mimicked easily on top of this type
>of operatin system.

Yes, you're absolutely right if you include the ability to track my
resource usage as well as protect me from other programs that
may behave aberrantly.  Very good Carl, you're starting to learn something about
operatings systems.  Now, what's your point in the above paragraph?

>Ditto.  Why should an operating system have compilers, for example?  Because
>the functionality is useful.

"Ditto.  Why should an operating system have {games/editors/newsreaders/etc?"

Here's a clue, Carl: Operating systems support compilers, they don't have them.

>As I've pointed out time and again, and folks like you STILL don't understand,
>IF YOU WANT TO ACCESS A FILE AS A STREAM OF BYTES, RMS WILL LET YOU DO THAT. 
>What the hell is your problem?  If you don't want the extra functionality,
>don't use it.  It's a simple as that.  SHEESH!

Carl, I don't have a problem.  I understand that RMS will allow me to access
a file as a stream of bytes.  My question is:  Why does it have any other
modes and why is it so deeply ingrained in VMS?  I don't want to access a file
in any way BUT as a stream of bytes - why must I muck with RMS to
do even that?

>No, instead folks using Unix implement the equivalent of various subsets of RMS
>time and time again, and most of those implementations are mutually
>incompatible.

That they feel compelled to do this illustrates more the futility of creating
an end-all-and-be-all like RMS than it does any inherent design defect
vis-a-vis UNIX's flexibility.

If there were only one, good, way of doing an "RMS" type thingy,
you would think that there WOULD be a library for it.  After all, we've
settled on a standard I/O library in the past, as well as standard
DBMS libraries, and math libraries, to name a few.

>Disclaimer:  Hey, I understand VAXen and VMS.  That's what I get paid for.  My
>understanding of astronomy is purely at the amateur level (or below).  So
>unless what I'm saying is directly related to VAX/VMS, don't hold me or my
>organization responsible for it.  If it IS related to VAX/VMS, you can try to
>hold me responsible for it, but my organization had nothing to do with it.

Unfortunately you, like David Cutler, don't understand a thing about
operating systems.

Software stands between the user and the machine, Carl.

greg


