I must admit I did not follow this thread for long, and therefore deserve some 
heat.  I'd chalk it up as a learning experience except for your response and 
some posts I've read since which contain a disturbing hatred toward making 
things easy for the user community.  The majority of computers are not 
purchased because a bunch of wires shoving electrons around to some superb 
master plan is a neat idea; they are bought to *solve problems* that take too 
long/are too hard to do by hand. A "technically perfect" OS that's too hard for
its intended customers to use doesn't do much good. Ignore your customers (read 
"users") at the peril of your profits.  Your contempt is puzzling considering 
your business:
># Automatic Data Processing, Dealer Services Division. ksg@plaza.ds.adp.com #
                              ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^  

>: I've been able to hack around and use the on-line help to do quite a
>: lot, whereas trying to learn something as easy as including a file
>: in an e-mail reply using UNIX is like learning Braille Sanskrit.
>	There is an old Internet saying: RTFM Read the FUCKING Manual

The old saying only applies *if you have access to a manual*, something that 
many users in the business world don't have for all the various OS's they use 
since corporate budgets only usually only allow for SYSADMIN types to get 
those.  That's why comprehensive but easy to use on-line help is critical.  And
most printed manuals are so poorly done with skimpy indexes that they are 
useless in finding anything but the most top level info.  Finally, given this 
group is part of a world-wide storehouse of electonic info, from where *can* I
FTP a good UNIX Users Manual along the lines of "UNIX for the Impatient"?

>But then there is the ignorance factor! People who have had minimal
>experience with UNIX and almost no experience with VMS trying to tell
>the world which operating system is better, and trying to pass of as
>an expert ( Why in the hell do we have *.advocacy groups??? )!!!!

If your implying that I hold my opinions (which were stated as such) re: the 
unfriendliness of UNIX because of lack of computer experience; I've been using 
computers to solve engineering problems for 18 years on systems such as Cray 
(using their UNIX OS since 1989), CDC, IBM (mainframes, workstations, PCs), 
Mac, PDP, Sun, and VAX.  *In my opinion* once again (and running the risk of 
alienating those who've helped me on the vms forum), with the exception of full-
screen editing there have been very few "improvements" in *any* OS I've used,
including VMS, that have really made my life easier in creating NASTRAN data 
decks, running jobs, and looking at output (which is what I get paid for) since
I stopped having to use punch cards.  Perhaps the problem is computer "experts"
deciding what systems the "users" get stuck with; I know many recommedations
over the years from fellow engineers have been ignored in favor of more 
*powerfull* systems  :-(

BTW, I'm not the only one ever to hold that opinion about UNIX; from 
the August 1989 issue of "Bit Bucket" by Grumman Data Systems System 
Engineering: "(UNICOS) Release 4.0 did not contain many of the features 
necessary for COS users to convert comfortably".  And other systems have their
own problems, witness my recent plea for help in column editing on a VAX (the
solution is much more complicated than on a CDC or IBM's TSO/ISPF editor).

You may flame me regarding my personal opinions, but I doubt they'll change.

>This guy is no rocket scientest!!! .....  uh, perhaps I was wrong....
No, you were correct. I'm an engineer, not a scientist.  There is a difference.

