|> 
|> That no one has done this may have more to do with the inherent
|> bogosity of RMS than any particular limitation of Unix.

	I'm not sure it's been mentioned, so I'll do so. That somewhat
heavyweight layer of software between the application and the disks
does a fair bit more than just impose structure on the file. It also
provides cluster-wide management facilities. The horrid things NFS does
to you when you assume that a filesystem somewhere else behaves the
same as a filesystem here simply do not occur.

	If you've never had your head ripped off by the joys of Unix
files on Unix clusters, you probably haven't tried to make a bunch of
boxes work on one job. These issues simply don't exist on a VMS cluster.

	From my point of view the most important distinction is that
a VAX cluster gives you *guarantees* on filesystem semantics. An
NFS based cluster tends to give you a really darn good chance that it'll
work right. This is the distinction between bet-your-business production
systems and academic toys, where you can just shrug, and crank it up
again.

	Andrew Molitor
	not speaking for NSC

