	# This is poorly stated [e.g. what about cc?].

Yes, I did state it rather poorly.  See my reply to Carl Lydick for a
better explanation.

	# cc calls a variety of sub-programs, but it calls them as
	# modules.

Agreed.  Plus, when you run "cc" you use the "cc" interface (a zillion
command line switches and all).  "cc -c" does not end up presenting
you with a different interface than "cc -g".

	# SET or ANALYZE seem to be nothing more than a giant switch
	# statement.  This implies something about the mental model
	# one must build to understand each of these commands.

Yes.  VMS hides the executables underneath a command language, and a
given command verb (e.g. ANALYZE) can end up invoking different
programs with varying interfaces.  I think the "mental model[s]" of
Unix and VMS commands are fairly different, although both are equally
valid.  For some (many? most?), VMS' model is preferable.

	# In my opinion, unix provides a much higher level of
	# modularity than VMS.  I'm not sure how to quantify this.

Possibly because Unix takes a plug-and-play approach to programming.
An executable binary image is only one of many types of programs --
others include shell scripts (pick your favorite shell language), awk
(for record processing), sed (for stream editing), perl (for system
management programing), and whatever else you want to add to the
environment.  All programs are run with the same basic mechanism (in
contrast to VMS, where you can RUN an image, @ a script,
FOO:==$BAR.EXE for a foreign command and then say FOO to run it,
etc.).  Plus, Unix provides basic concepts/mechanisms in its
command-line user interface (shells) to allow "pipes" of different
commands where the stream-output of one program flows into the
stream-input of another.

Note that one can do similar things in VMS (there are many languages
available), but it isn't as smooth (in my humble opinion).  Executable
images are treated differently from command scripts.  Command language
(i.e. .CLD files) work differently from foreign commands.  Pipes
aren't provided in basic VMS (although they can be retrofitted to some
extent with PD utilities).  Stream input and stream output as concepts
have not been applied to as many areas: one typically uses RMS to
access files, rather than treating file I/O as a stream of bytes (i.e.
file I/O and program input/output in Unix are very similar, and
interchangable in many cases).  [Yes, yes, VMS does allow files to be
treated as streams, but this is not as commonly used as in Unix.]  VMS
cetainly has some great programming tools, but how they fit together
is different than Unix.

A caveat: this Unix view is great for programmers (especially C and shell
programmers).  It is lacking somewhat for general users who don't know
as much about computers.

