In Article <1994Feb26.204059.4068@mixcom.mixcom.com>
Craig Dedo <Craig.Dedo@mixcom.mixcom.com> writes:

>  1. Does VMS have much of a future?  

Probably.  It is a mature, robust, highly functional operating system
with a large user base.   It has largely held on to its conceptual
integrity over nearly 20 years of extensive development, i.e., we can
expect further development.

VMS has never been in the mass desktop market (current entry-point for
a desktop machine with VMS is still about $US 5000 I believe).
This may change (e.g., if super-low cost AXP's come out), but it
doesn't have to.  Desk-top machines will always need servers and VMS
systems fit this market well (as they always have).

Many other followups have expressed not only the contrary view to the
above, but have also stated that VMS is *already* dying.  None of
these people quote any statistics to back up this latter claim, hence
they can all be dismissed as bigots blowing hot air.

>  2. Is VMS losing out to Unix?  If so, why?  If not, what evidence is
>there that this is not the case?

This is a really crucial question.  Crucial, because it is
nonsensical, being based on a false premise.  VMS is Unix.  Dozens of
other followups have also made the assumption that VMS is not Unix,
and hence the arguments that they present are largely gibberish.

For the doubters: how do you decide whether an operating system is
Unix or non-Unix?  I say there is only one sensible answer: by
conformance to recognised Unix standard(s).  VMS already conforms to
all the international standards that have any claim to defining Unix,
hence VMS is Unix.

>  3. Does VMS provide enough bang for the buck?  How does it compare to
>the competition in this area?

Probably.  The AXP machines have impressive raw grunt, but I'd like
to see some *sophisticated* benchmarks that demonstrate how this
translates to system performance.

>
>  4. What do you think are the strengths of VMS that would make it an
>attractive alternative to someone who is used to Unix?  To MS-DOS?

I can't see it ever competing with MS-DOS, because I don't see VMS
ever running on Intel hardware.

>  5. If the big objection to VMS is that it is proprietary, why don't we
>hear such complaints about MS-DOS and Windows?

Because the MS-DOS and MS-Windows user would see anyone saying this as the
morons that they are (just like if they were told that there motor
cars were no good for the same reason).  It is only in a certain
sub-culture of the Unix market where such an attitude is regarded as
admirable, rather than as nonsensical hypocrisy.

>  6. Should DEC consider franchising VMS to independent software
>developers (ISVs) who could add their own enhancements, the way Microsoft
>has done with MS-DOS or McDonald's has done with their restaurants?  Would
>this answer the objection that VMS is proprietary while Unix is not?

No.  VMS is Unix.  All Unix operating systems that I know of (except
for linux) are proprietary.  This is not a problem.

>  7. How can DEC make VMS attractive to the nation's computer science
>departments?

Stress to them that is is Unix :-)/2

>  8. How can DEC make VMS attractive to software developers?

I thought they already had.

>  9. Does VMS have features that are not found or are not nearly as well
>developed in competing operating systems?  If so, what are they?  

Several people have mentioned a lot of features.  I'll reiterate
VMSclusters and the robust, distributable file system as the jewels
in the crown.

>  10.  Are these features (which are better developed in VMS) very much
>appreciated by the people who actually make the operating system selection
>decisions?  If not, why not?

From personal experience I can safely say ... sometimes.  You'll need
some detailed research into the decision making processes of a large
sample of enerprises to say any more than this.

>  11.  What market should VMS serve?

It's traditional base --- the server market is the obvious answer. 

Penetrating the desktop market to any great extent would require new
ultra-low-cost hardware, and a big marketing push.  It is a long shot
but not impossible.  DEC already have an Alpha based PC.  If DEC have
an agressively priced model that can run OSF/1, OS/2, VMS, or
MS-Windows equally well, then it is not beyond the bounds of
possibility that it would make major market penetration, and hence
give VMS the opportunity to follow (not that it has to --- the server
market will remain a respectable niche).

>  12.  Should DEC develop a standard GUI interface to VMS, such as
>Microsoft Windows or X-Windows?

They've had the multi-vendor standard Motif interface for VMS for years.


