In article <ROCKWELL.94Mar7223516@nova.umd.edu>, rockwell@nova.umd.edu (Raul Deluth Miller) writes:
> Chris Lishka:
> 	   * Program-centerred command syntax.  VMS uses a mechanism
> 	     to hide the actual programs being run from the user.  For
> 	     example, the "SET" and "ANALYZE" commands actually run
> 	     several different executables depending on what you
> 	     choose (i.e. "ANALYZE DISK" vs. "ANALYZE CRASH").  
> 
> This is poorly stated [e.g. what about cc?].
> 
> In my opinion, unix provides a much higher level of modularity than
> VMS.  I'm not sure how to quantify this.  I know it's not even true
> for the system administrator.  I suspect that the system administrator
> has an easier time of it on VMS because users are more restricted.  Of
> course, VMS is so well designed that users don't have any reason to
> have the restrictions lifted...

	Not a higher level of modularity, it's just that the modularity
is divided across different lines.

	With Unix, you have a pretty sharp division between the shell and
the program.

	With VMS, there is a division between DCL and the command definition
and then again at the program.  There are defined interfaces between them,
though they are different than the interfaces with Unix (CLI$ routine calls
rather than getenv() and argc/argv).  Making the command syntax a separate
module from the program allows you to change the command syntax without
changing the program.

> Back to cc -- cc calls a variety of sub-programs, but it calls them as
> modules.  SET or ANALYZE seem to be nothing more than a giant switch
> statement.  This implies something about the mental model one must
> build to understand each of these commands.

	Basically, you're correct.  But even in Unix, most commands have an
actual case/switch statement in them to parse their options as well.  The shell
has to hash the command (again, a different type of switch) to determine
which/where the program to run is.  It's just a matter of where the switch is,
but under any O/S, some switch/hash/lookup must be done.  The mental model is
indeed differnt, but that not make one superior to the other.

