Chris Lishka (lishka@dxcern.cern.ch) wrote:


: The environment that the DEC developers created for all of ANALYZE's
: facets is not consistent.  If you ANALYZE/DISK, you use an interface
: based on command line options.  If you ANALYZE/CRASH or
: ANALYZE/SYSTEM, you use a command oriented shell like interface
: similar to a debugger.  Similarily, SHOW SYMBOL uses a command line
: interface.  SHOW CLUSTER also uses a command line interface, except if
: you use SHOW CLUSTER/CONTINOUS, which throws one into a command
: oriented shell interface.  Some commands like LICENSE or AUTHORIZE
: seem to be a single program (with a single interface).  Other programs
: like SHOW, ANALYZE, or DELETE seem to be many programs with many
: purposes (e.g. DELETE works with removing files, logicals, symbols,
: queue entries, queues, intrusion records, etc.).  In VMS, there is a
: definite seperation between the command you give (verbs, modifiers,
: etc.) and the program that is run.

: What I like about Unix is that 95% of the time (or more) the command I
: run has a single interface.  I don't have to guess that lying
: underneath the ANALYZE/CRASH program is the System Dump Analyzer
: (SDA), yet lying under ANALYZE/MEDIA is the Bad Block Locator (which
: are documented seperately), and ANALYZE/DISK is yet another program.

Yup.  Some might say "But look VMS is closer to english and so easier for
new users."  But does that matter?  In my opinion, both UNIX and VMS are so
far from english *anyway*, that it comes down to pure memorization and
physical 'finger-feel' in both cases---and it's easier to do and use with
Unix.

A really naive user would be stuck just in some app or on a Mac anyway.

------------------------------

The canonical example:

VMS

$ SHOW USERS

Unix

% who

So, VMS *seems* more logical.

-------------------------------

Now look at Joe Sixpack,

sixpack: "Ok, I want to see what my files are."

$ SHOW FILES

"nope"

$ TYPE FILES

"uh uh."

$ DISPLAY NAMES/FILE

"Aw comeon".

$ LIST FILENAMES

dammit.

$ *.*/DISPLAY

argh.

$ PRINT FILE DIRECTORY

"Oh forget it.  Hey syadmin, How do I get a list of my files?"

sysadmin:"DIR." 
Sixpack: "Hu-what?" 

sysadmin: (condescendingly), "It's just like English, you know, Dee-Eye-Aar
for *diiiirectory**??"

(OK, I exaggerate)

: I personally feel that VMS verbs/commands are overloaded in their
: functionality.  You are free to feel otherwise.  I prefer the Unix
: way. 

DCL tries to be superficially "smart" even though it is at its core, still
just as stupid as any other shell.  

Unix doesn't arrogantly make that supposition and just tries to do it's
lesser job well. 

Unix also has some simple attractive 'ideas' (shell is
just a program, stdin and stdout).  What is the 'gestalt' for VMS?  

I bet the netnews routing software you're seeing this message on is
on Unix.

:  I hear the endless murmur,                               Christopher Lishka

My personal impression is that VMS is an OS that some people really want
to make *other* people use.

