David L. Cathey:
.  > .	   For example: grep takes one search key followed by a list
.  > .  of file names.  Why not multiple search keys?  

Raul Deluth Miller:
.  > egrep 'key1|key2|key3' files

David L. Cathey:
.  Darn...  I wanted to search for |'s but now the syntax is going to
.  get strange, I bet...  But that's okay, since it's alright for Unix
.  to do that, but DCL must still be broken when it get's strange with
.  "s...

Huh?

There's a batch of different quoting characters under shell/egrep:
  ''           quote everything between these.
  ""           quote everything between these, but do variable substitutions. 
  \            quote the next character.
  []           this is a set of characters.

The only "confusing" issue is that both the shell and egrep deal with
meta-character handling.  In my above example, I've assumed that
meta-character handling is enabled in the shell.  If you turn it off
in the shell, you search for a single key with | in it.

The only reason this is "confusing" is that you have so many options
to pick from.  This is balanced by the fact that these options let you
do a wide variety of things [in this case, you are allowed to look for
a wide variety of patterns].  Most of the time, people just want to
look for alphanumeric keys -- and the options may be ignored.

Also, note that the above syntax works with files-in-a-file-system, as
well as with disk and tape devices and executables and so on.  This
might also be considered confusing -- it allows you a lot of options
that you might not wish to excercise at any particular instance.  Most
of the time, people have these things organized in a reasonable
directory structure -- and the options may be ignored.

.  Notice that because of the interaction of the shell, the search key
.  must be quoted to be within one argument.  Also, what if you want
.  to arrange the aruments differently?  Given your average shell, you
.  are constrained in your design of your command line.

What are you asking here?  How does "arranging the arguments
differently" accomplish any purpose?

