In article <2l7pbn$kk9@gap.cco.caltech.edu> carl@SOL1.GPS.CALTECH.EDU writes:
)The point is that the shell technique for expanding wildcards requires that all
)instances of the expansion be placed in the argument list.  I've yet to see a
)shell that didn't have a hard-coded limit to the size of the argument list
)(yeah, I know, you don't have to have one;  you COULD make a guess as to how
)many arguments you're going to generate, malloc space for pointers to them, if
)you exceed the estimate, malloc more space, copy the already generated
)pointers, and iterate).

	In most UNIX's, the arg space limit is not truly limited by the shell,
but by the kernel. Basically the kernel has to put that arg list somewhere
before performing the exec() call. It is data being transfered from the old
program image to the new.  In PDP-11 days it used to be terribly
restricted (I believe it used a filesystem buffer, 512bytes). Most UNIX's
these days have limits like 10-20Kb. I suppose a better alternative would be
to save the old process memory space while doing the exec and copy the args
from that memory to the new address space before destroying the old image.
However, then you would end up with situations like having to pagein the args
if there were too many, or not being able to exec a 100Mb program from a 100Mb
program if your swap space were 120Mb.


