>You will also notice that when these ``microkernels'' come out that they
>are orders of magnitude bigger than older ``monoltical'' kernels.
>Spring OS, as far as I can tell, not a product, not is it meant to be,
>at this time.

One example counter to this is QNX.  The QNX microkernel is 10 Kbytes in 
size, surrounded by a team of cooperating processes (all running in 
MMU-protected address spaces) that provide higher-level OS services.  
Device drivers, file systems, character device I/O, networking, etc are all 
external processes.  Although architecturally very different from 
monolithic-kernel UNIX, it is POSIX-certified.

>>Microkernels in addition, allow you to more easily run a distributed
>>computing environment. The other value added feature of Microkernels
>>is that they allow bits of the OS to be easily `slotted' in and out.
>>This is simply because the interface between the parts is very
>>well defined.
>
>You can do that just as easily in other, non-microkernel, OSes.

I don't know of any monolithic kernels that let me, from a networked node, 
slay my filesystem process (my one and only), and start another one.  Or 
run multiple of them side-by-side.  Or do the same with my character device 
I/O subsystem, or networking, or all the device drivers for those 
subsystems as well.

>That's what it's all about: a well defined interface.  The microkernel
>is nice as a model, a concept.  It is also nice that it forces
>a modular structure in your kernel  I believe that certain concepts
>of the microkernel (such as a very modular, well structured kernel)
>will survive.  The implementation model will have a much harder time
>gaining ground and surviving other than in limited circumstances.

Please define these limited circumstances and why a microkernel doesn't 
fit.  :-)

>Modulo the rather unfortunate ``buzzword'' craze that is damaging
>our industry.

True - some microkernel vendors have taken to calling their products
"nanokernel" in order to differentiate themselves from other products
with "not-so-micro-kernels".  :-)


