> 
> 	- WINE/DOS emulation under Linux
> 	- Wabi/SunPC/SoftPC under SunOS/Solaris/Other Unices.
> 
Yes you can. But the difference is that under a microkernel you
should be able to run multiple operating systems in a much
more transparent way ie they are running as operating systems
as opposed to simply being emulated applications. 
But granted it's a small difference. I see the advantages of
microkernels as more being to the OS implementer than anyone
else. 

> 
> Windows NT is not a micro-kernel, that's where marketing steps in.
> Windows NT is also the system where people ask: I can run use WIN32
> or POSIX or whatever in my program BUT NOT AT THE SAME TIME.
> People rather have a the full combination of features than
> (disjunct) sets.

I would agree that NT is not a microkernel but it is all a matter of 
opinion. To me a microkernel should consist only of the parts of
the OS that require to run in non user modes. Everything else
should run in user mode. So it depends on how much of an OS
you consider deserves to be included in the microkernel.
   
> 
> >In addition, IBM is set to release Workplace OS on the Power PC and this
> >is also a microkernel. I think you will struggle to find a major OS vendor
> >that is not intending to microkernel their OS and to place 
> >objects on top of this. SUN with Spring OS, Apple/IBM with taligent.
> >This is not in the distant future this is within the next few years (2/3 years).
> 
