A question for Carl:

	It appears obvious, to me, that a stream file - i.e. an indexed
on-dimensional array of arbitrary bytes (as is available with
lseek/read/write in Unix) is THE atomic form of a file.  All other
file structures can be imposed/mimicked easily on top of this type
of structure.

If that's all that the operating system has to support then why, in
God's name, should it support others?  It seems totally counter to
the idea that "software stands between the user and the machine, therefore
enforce as few restrictions in software as possible" to impose any
additional structure, at the system level, on a file.

It would be possible to implement RMS's functionality on UNIX via a
libRMS.a mechanism.  That way the code for RMS is promoted to the
application level, while leaving the system itself lean 'n mean.

That no one has done this may have more to do with the inherent
bogosity of RMS than any particular limitation of Unix.

greg


