In article <1994Mar7.142957.18455@dxcern.cern.ch>, lishka@dxcern.cern.ch (Chris Lishka) writes:
=Six of one, half dozen of the other.  I >like< the fact that Unix
=makes no attempt to force a tape into the filesystem view.  In Unix a
=tape is a tape.  In VMS, a tape is a simple ANSI tape if mounted
=foreign, or a really bizarre subset of FILES-11 if mounted normally.

No.  If mounted foreign it's a simple tape.  It need not have ANSI labels.  If
mounted normally, it's a simple ANSI tape whose files VMS refers to using
FILES-11 syntax.  Dealing with ANSI tapes is a relatively new development for
Unix. 

=Certainly.  Here "DEFINE/USER SYS$OUTPUT ..." works fine.
=
=	$
=	$ create zfoo.txt
=	1
=	2
=	3
=	*EXIT*
=	$ open/append myfile zfoo.txt
=	$ define/user sys$output myfile
=	$ dir
=	$ close myfile
=	$ type zfoo.txt
=	1
=	2
=	3
=
=	Directory DISK$USER:[TESTUSER]
=
=	LOGIN.COM;1         MAIL.MAI;1          ZFOO.TXT;1
=
=	Total of 3 files.
=	$
=
=Here, "DEFINE SYS$OUTPUT ..." doesn't seem to work as I would expect
=(based on the DEFINE/USER example above):
=
=	$ create zfoo.txt
=	4
=	5
=	6
=	$ open/append myfile zfoo.txt
=	$ define sys$output myfile
=	%DCL-E-OPENOUT, error opening MYFILE:.; as output
=	-RMS-F-RAT, invalid record attributes
=	$ close myfile
=
=Why is there a difference in how DEFINE/USER and DEFINE treat the
=second parameter?

Because defining SYS$OUTPUT in supervisor mode creates the file.  Defining it
in user mode simply causes a logical name definition.  A user-mode logical name
lasts only until the next image termination.  User-mode logical names are
primarily intended for use by images.  A supervisor-mode logical name stays
around until you specifically deassign the logical name.  For a supervisor mode
definition of SYS$OUTPUT to work properly, DCL must open the file;  otherwise
you'd be opening a different file for each image you invoke.  For a user-mode
definition, the image opens the file.
=Another example.  The following doesn't work:
=
=	$ dir
=	%DIRECT-W-NOFILES, no files found
=	$ create testme.com
=	$ write sys$output "Inside testme.com"
=	 Exit
=	$ define/user sys$output zfoo.txt
=	$ @testme
=	%DCL-W-UNDFIL, file has not been opened by DCL - check logical name
=	$ dir
=
=	Directory SCRATCH$DAY:[TESTUSER]
=
=	TESTME.COM;1        ZFOO.TXT;1
=
=	Total of 2 files.
=	$ type zfoo.txt
=	%DCL-W-UNDFIL, file has not been opened by DCL - check logical name
=	$

Again, because you've defined a user-mode logical name, DCL doesn't immediately
create the file.  However, when it has to write to SYS$OUTPUT to deliver the
error message, that forces it to open the file.

=However, this does work:
=
=	$ dir
=	%DIRECT-W-NOFILES, no files found
=	$ def/user sys$output zbar.txt
=	$ dir
=	$ dir
=
=	Directory SCRATCH$DAY:[TESTUSER]
=
=	ZBAR.TXT;1
=
=	Total of 1 file.
=	$ type zbar.txt
=
=	Directory SCRATCH$DAY:[TESTUSER]
=
=	ZBAR.TXT;1
=
=	Total of 1 file.
=	$

In that case, DIRECTORY opens the file pointed to by the user-mode definition
of SYS$OUTPUT.
=Presumably the first case didn't work because the file zfoo.txt had
=not been explicitly opened by my DCL process for writing.

Correct.

