In <2lg2t2$pnu@agate.berkeley.edu> avk@hafnium.cchem.berkeley.edu (Tony Konashenok) writes:

>You are forgetting that your "atomic" form is NOT the way data actually
>exist on media. As we all know, data are broken into records, and it's a
>record that is handled by OS's I/O routines, not a byte (except terminals
>and similar devices).

Yes, but so what?  Many different devices have all kinds of different
record sizes.  Some, like terminals, have no record size at all (usually).

It's the operating system's job to abstract device differences away
from the user.  After all, record-formatting at the disk level is
not done for any particular philosophic reason, it's just a compromise
due to our inability to manufacture main memory cheaply!

>So, why should "the only form of a file" be a 1-D
>array of bytes rather than an array of 512-byte blocks? (Or any other
>size, maybe even variable, as in IBM mainframe disks).

Umm, is the answer because I can easily imitate arrays of blocks if you
give me a 1-D array of bytes?  It's easier to simulate n-dimensional
memory from 1-dimensional memory than it is the other way around.  And,
since one of the operating system's jobs should be to make our life easier,
why not have it present everything as a 1-D stream of bytes instead of
n-D blocks of bytes?

Why do you want to know how the device stores information, anyway?
Afterall, it's likely to change in the future.

greg


