   programmer-centric, since your average computer user doesn't want
   to program, but get their work done.  This is one reason why the
   desktop of choice is MS-DOS, an "O/S" that vastly outnumbers Unix
   as well as VMS.

Nope.  The reason is that Microsoft has a contract with most of the
OEMs where they ship a copy of DOS with every machine.  DOS comes
pre-installed.  That's all there is to it.

   > (2) Wildcards.  It's true that you could get a Unix shell to do
   > it the way DCL does, but this would be a mistake.

	   In this I would agree, since almost every file-oriented
   command on Unix would break or become much less useful because of
   their inability to understand wildcards.

The unix was of dealing with this [a shell without
file-in-this-directory wildcard processing] would be to build a
utility that does the processing.  If the shell also couldn't deal
with using the output of a command as the argument for some other
command then a utility could be written for that.

So, basically, under unix, if you removed functionality from one
routine [the shell] it would need to go in another routine.  Doesn't
sound like a very big issue to me.

	   Again, counter-arguments to Unix wild-carding have also
   been made, since if forces the design of the utility to work with
   what the shell is able to give it in terms of files.  

?

No force involved, the shell can pass arbitrary strings.  Most people
live without control characters in their arguments, however.

   Another oddity is that the shell only interprets wildcarded
   filenames for a single directory.  However, if a recursive
   directory lookup is needed, the utility has to be able to perform
   that itself (provide its own wildcarding) since it is incompletly
   done by the shell.

Another oddity is that the shell doesn't provide menuing, so if
menuing is desired either (a) it has to go in the program or (b) a
general utility has to be provided.  Then again, it only takes a few
lines of code to, for example, turn vi into a menu system.

