        I really don't understand the  persistence of this question.  By
    default,  HELP  _does_ output a page at a time.  There are only  two
    ways to avoid it: (1) type $HELP/NOPAGE,  or  (2)  you've  got  your
    terminal  PAGE setting wrong.  Gee, even "man" and "more" won't work
    if the stty settings are wrong.   Granted, terminal emulators can be
    a PIA (I've worked with a bunch of them), but this isn't an emulator
    problem per se.

        And in a later note in this  thread, Raul asked about just being
    able  to get the HELP text into a file.  Well, gee Raul, if you know
    how to do "man man", why don't you give "HELP HELP" a try?   If  you
    had,  you'd  encounter  the /OUTPUT qualifier which allows you to do
    exactly what you want.  You can  even  get the entire hierarchy on a
    help entry by using a wild card, e.g.

        	$ HELP/OUTPUT=PRINT.HELP PRINT  *  

    The use of the wildcard is documented in HELP HELP, BTW.  And if you
    think that /OUTPUT is somehow  curious,  unusual, or "I have to know
    it's  there  before  I can ask about it", I'd just  point  out  that
    /OUTPUT is one of the most ubiquitous qualifiers you'll find in DCL.
    Essentially any command for which you might want to redirect  output
    to  a  file  uses  the  same /OUTPUT=filespec syntax, so once you've
    learned it for HELP,  you've  learned it for DIRECTORY, DIFFERENCES,
    SHOW LOGICAL, etc., etc.

        'Nuff said..

        	-Ken
