> In article <1994Mar5.214319.4483@belvedere.sbay.org>, root@belvedere.sbay.org (David E. Fox) writes:
> =Carl J Lydick (carl@SOL1.GPS.CALTECH.EDU) wrote:
> =
> =: Also, what that Unixism means is that each person creates his own tle
> =: structure, generally incompatible with everybody else's file structures.
> =
> =No, what "Unixism" means is that for the large part files are stored as
> =flat ascii sequential files, which are eminently portable.
> 
> As I've said time and again, but you're apparently just too damned stupid to
> understand:  If you want a file as a stream of bytes, RMS lets you do that. 

I think the argument was not that porting files TO RMS was that difficult
(it isn't), but that porting files FROM RMS could be difficult (which it
certainly can be, if the target system doesn't support, eg, indexed files
and if you want the ported file to have an indexed organization).

In any event, the argumeent that Unix files are inherently more portable
than VMS files is not true, except when you limit the target systems to
other Unix or VMS or MS-DOS machines.  Many mainframe operating systems
cannot deal with files as a stream of bytes (or at least not without your
having to rewrite all of your existing file handling code from the ground
up - it's not supported by any of the language I/O nor by any of the OS
utilities;  not my idea of an "easy port":  yeah, so you get the file
over there, and have _NO_ programs that can read it, and every program
you want to use on the file has to be rewritten in order to handle it).

Of course, these mainframe OS's are in even greater danger of extinction
than is VMS ...

In any event, the technical issues are _irrelevant_.  VMS has lost the
marketing war, and the only question now is whether Unix will win out
in the long run over the likes of NT, Next, Pink and their relatives.
VMS is already out of the running, from purely market forces;  this
entire discussion is beside the point, because it is debating technical
details that have little or no bearing on market perception.

Bruce C. Wright


