> development is only a very small portion of real world software
> development going on.
> 
> Microkernels are intersting in OS reseach, they can be intersting in
> certain real-time applications, but their disadvantages outweigh
> their advantages for a general purprose operating system.

Not just research I am afraid. Already Windows NT is implemented as a 
microkernel according to Microsoft (but that's another flame war).
In addition, IBM is set to release Workplace OS on the Power PC and this
is also a microkernel. I think you will struggle to find a major OS vendor
that is not intending to microkernel their OS and to place 
objects on top of this. SUN with Spring OS, Apple/IBM with taligent.
This is not in the distant future this is within the next few years (2/3 years).
And in the case of Workplace OS this Year.

In the case of NextStep it is frequently true that the first ground breaking
product is not the one that succeeds. Take Windows and Macs as a classic
example.

Microkernels in addition, allow you to more easily run a distributed
computing environment. The other value added feature of Microkernels
is that they allow bits of the OS to be easily `slotted' in and out.
This is simply because the interface between the parts is very
well defined.

