In article <1994Mar6.115738.6527@ais.com>, bruce@ais.com (Bruce C. Wright) writes:
=> As I've said time and again, but you're apparently just too damned stupid to
=> understand:  If you want a file as a stream of bytes, RMS lets you do that. 
=
=I think the argument was not that porting files TO RMS was that difficult
=(it isn't), but that porting files FROM RMS could be difficult (which it
=certainly can be, if the target system doesn't support, eg, indexed files
=and if you want the ported file to have an indexed organization).

Fine.  If you want the ported file to have an indexed organization, then write
your own software to support that sort of file organization (because Unix
doesn't have any standard software to do it), and issue the following commands
on your VMS system:
	$ CONVERT/FDL=SYS$INPUT: old_file new_file
	RECORD
		FORM	STREAM_LF
You've now got a file that's "just a stream of bytes."  You can do with it
whatever you want on the Unix system.  One DCL command, two lines of
instructions to that command, and you've got the file in the only format Unix
supports.  Should take you under 30 seconds to enter that command.  You can let
the command run while you spend the hours it will take to write the software to
support an ISAM file on the Unix system.  Chances are, the convert operation on
the VMS side will have finished long before you're even ready to try your first
compilation of your home-grown software on the Unix side.

The argument has been that, somehow, RMS is bad.  Since one can trivially
convert a file from any RMS structure to the ONE structure supported by Unix,
the claim is utterly bogus.  If you want to restrict yourself to stream files
under VMS, you can do so.  If you want to take advantage of the additional
support RMS gives you, you can do that too.  Under Unix, you don't have the
choice.
