In article <ROCKWELL.94Mar4103735@nova.umd.edu>, rockwell@nova.umd.edu (Raul
Deluth Miller) writes:

>.  To list the contents of the current directory and all subdirectories:
>	   $ DIRECTORY [...]
>.  To list the contents of all directories on the disk other than the main
>file
>.  directory:
>	   $ DIRECTORY [*...]
>.  To list the contents of all directories on the disk:
>	   $ DIRECTORY [000000...]
>
>I presume the three dots are to be taken literally?  And it's six
>zeros for that last command?  Thank you.

Yup.  ... is the VMS-ism for "give me the directory tree from the current
directory down"

000000 is the root directory on a Files-11, ODS-2 disk, often referred to in
VMS culture as the master file directory or the MFD.

>One more question?  How do I get a list of all disks?  For that
>matter, how do I get a list of all configured devices?  [What do I
>need that information for?  Mostly, curiosity.  I can't very well talk
>about a subject intelligently when I have no experience with that
>subject.]

See help show device

Show device will provide a list of all the devices the system currently
understands.  Which represent actual hardware, which are virtual, and which
are clones of devices takes a little more knowledge, that a curious user
could probably puzzle out.  A good rule of thumb is that disks begin with d,
ethernet devices with e, lat terminals with lt, terminals that are the result
of remote DECnet access are rt, directly connected terminals are tt.  This
is by no means a comprehensive list.

Show device with no argument gives you everybody, which on everything but
single-user desktop systems could be a rather substantial list (but most
DCL things can send their output to a file with the /output= qualifier).

Show dev device name gives a specific device, and you can partially specify
the device name and the command will return all the devices that match the
partial specification.


