	I would agree.  But such a statement admits that very few vendors
adhere to some "Open Standard".

> There's a concept of "a VMS sub-culture" which I was trying to
> address.  I was thinking that VMS could be made more attractive by
> addressing some of the social issues.  Perhaps I was wrong.

	There is a Unix sub-culture as well.  Hence the notion that much of
this is a "religious war" between operating systems.  I really doubt that
the social issues are caused by the operating systems so much as the people
themselves.

> Then again, I never encountered a piece of documentation in the
> on-line help [all that I could afford to access] which described the
> directory tree.  I remember trying various things to get that piece of
> information -- none worked.

	Two points on this:  VMS does not have a single "root" to the directory
tree.  Each mounted volume provides it's own tree (DOS does the same thing). 
Also, the "hierarchy" of files/directories is much like Unix, however, the
syntax is different than Unix or DOS.

>                              Perhaps, this was because the system
> denied my permission to see the top level directory [if there is such
> a thing].  Perhaps I just never comprehended how to list it.

	On some systems, the "top" level of a disk is read-protected:
You have to know that first directory to get there.  Again, it is not a
VMS issue, but set by the sysadmin (as if the sysadm did a chmod 661 /).

> However, if someone gives me an idea of what sort of command I could
> type in on a VMS system to get this piece of information, I'll write
> it down and save it -- in case I ever get back on a VMS system that
> happens to allow such an operation.

	pwd	=>	SHOW DEFAULT	(or SHO DEF for short)
	cd ..	=>	SET DEF [-]	(like subtracting a level)
	cd junk	=>	SET DEF [.JUNK] (note the '.')

	I often seen "CD :== SET DEFAULT" or "SD :== SET DFEAULT" to save
typing (much like an alias in csh).
 
> Hmm... you can do `man sh >tmp` and it works on unix much like it
> would work on DOS.  [If DOS had `man`].

	I was not referencing "man" in particular, but try to explain to a
DOS user about awk, grep, biff, sed, perl, ...

> Actually, I rather suspected this had something to do with the default
> configuration or with recommendations that accompanied some system
> doc.  These things are independent of the operating system code, but
> still accompany the operating system.

	There are some things that are default part of the configuration
(like forcing the sysadm to change SYSTEM, FIELD, etc account passwords).
There is a manual on System Management and Security.  You can make it as
secure as needed, but out-of-the-box it is very usable.  If you had a 
particular problem you were not happy with, I could tell you if this was
a intentional change or that way out-of-the-box.

> Once, I managed to figure out that something *had* to exist on the
> system, and I was thus able to form a specific request to the systemm
> administrator for this information.  In that case, I got a response
> where the system administrator changed the ACL and made the
> information available.  When I asked about other such areas, I was
> told to go buy a book on the subject.  [At that time, I was a student,
> and didn't have the money.]

	Without additional information, such a thing could easily be required
on a Unix system (again, you may have to ask where the information is and
can you get to it).  I've had to do this before on both systems, myself.

	For assistance, a book is nice (though the sysadm should have been
more helpful), but even on-line help can assist with many commands.
"$ HELP HINTS" can often provide insight into how many things work.

> I never found out enough about VMS to know what a class really is.

	A class is a site-determined set of processes that have a certain
amount (or whatever they want) of CPU allocated to them.  Default is all
processes use what they can get (VMS priority <=> Unix nice), much like
Unix process scheduling does as well.

> However, a system administrator is generally not going to know that a
> problem needs to be dealt with until the problem is made apparent.  A
> random student is going to learn a lot more about an operating system
> that was built with a "wide-open-share-everything" philosophy than
> about an operating system that was built with a "tighten down the
> hatches and lock the doors" philosophy.

	And here is where we see the difference.  Unix is a great system to
learn basics about operating systems function.  However, in industry, there
is a great need to "tighten down the hatches" for Defense, Banking,
Shop Floor, etc to prevent unauthorized access of resources and information.
When the data *is* your business, you want to protect it just like you lock
doors, hire guards, etc.

> One thing you can count on:  somebody is going to use the defaults.

	And for a system that businesses depend upon, those defaults should
provide for some moderate level of protection of the data stored on them.
But even still, most of the problems you seem to have had are getting to
documentation, or otherwise knowning how the system works.  VMS documentation
will tell you everything you would probably want to know, but you problem
is getting to it.  Not something any O/S can solve by itself.

