hardware controller successfully, but that controller's reply is dropped.
The read on the Alpha returns an empty string, but no error condition.
After a loss the system then works fine as if nothing had happened, though
often (perhaps always) the characters that should have been read turn up on
the next read.

It appears to be caused by the terminal server or reverse LAT in general,
because an older version of the system (uVAX II with serial port card) ran
fine. The Alpha reads using SYS$QIOW and both the returned status and IO
status block status are checked and are not returning an error.

The terminal server error counters and some configuration information have
been appended.

Are LAT terminal servers appropriate for this job (high-reliability reverse
LAT to 16 ports), or are we asking too much of them?

Are Lantronix terminal servers OK or should we buy some other brand?

Any suggestions for solving this problem would be most appreciated.

-- Russell
Russell E. Owen
owen@astro.washington.edu
University of Washington
Astronomy Dept. FM-20
Seattle, WA 98195
206-543-2859

Details:
- The Alpha connects via reverse LAT by node name and port name; the
terminal server does not offer any services other than its node name
- All ports are set to REMOTE access (meaning the Alpha must initiate the
connections)
- The Alpha and terminal server are on a dedicated subnet containing no
other devices. Communications with the one terminal server and a few telnet
sessions are all that are on the subnet. Data rates are on the order of 400
cps between the Alpha and the terminal server (but typically in bursts to
several ports at once) and less to each telnet session.

