In article <2kigri$84d@columba.udac.uu.se>, bqt@Update.UU.SE (Johnny Billquist) writes:
> 
> In <1994Feb22.130350.13227@hns.com> spreston@hns.com (Stephen Preston) writes:
> 
>>I am not sure if I am posting this question to the correct group.
> 
>>I have several RA90 disks that are constantly giving File Header Full error
>>messages.  There are 97,076 files on this disk.  I am pretty sure I understand
>>what the message is all about.  We have tried purges, defraggers, and 
>>backup/restores all with little luck. The problem will subside for a while but 
>>then quickly resurface.  Of course the long term solution is to move some 
>>accounts to another drive but disk space is a premium here as well.  How can 
>>the file header map area be monitored?  If we could predict when it would become
>>full, we may be able to take corrective measures before our customers are 
>>impacted.
> 
> The area for file headers can be extended to allow more of them.
> I don't know the command for this under VMS, but look in the
> manuals under disk initialization. It should hopefully also
> contain information on how to change this without re-initializing
> the disk.
> If you're talking RSX, you use the HOM command.

Generally this means the index file's file header is full on this disk,
and you will not be able to add more files without reinitializing.

To see index file header fragmentation (the reason for the problem), do

  $ DUMP/HEADER ddnn:[000000]INDEXF.SYS /BLOCK=COUNT:0

As I recall, you can get on the order of 60 fragments in an index file
header before it fills up (not a fixed value).

You can defragment the index file's header by doing an image backup/restore
of the disk.  If you want to preallocate extra headers to try to keep the
problem from happening in the future, initialize the destination volume
with something like

  $ INIT ddxx:/HEADERS=150000 ...

and copy your old files to that disk with BACKUP/IMAGE/NOINITIALIZE .
(Warning: /IMAGE/NOINIT was broken at roughly VMS 5.2, but it works
again as of VMS 5.5).  Note that every preallocated header uses up one
disk block.  You may also be interested in the /MAXIMUM_FILES qualifier.

