In article <1994Mar4.173847.22634@rhrk.uni-kl.de>, dhaase@unix-ag.uni-kl.de (David Haase) writes:
> Hello,
> 
> I've just found an Emulex CS11 communications multiplexer that was built into
> a VAX. It has two modules with eight serial outputs each, a power supply and
> two connectors on the back, which are labelled 'Data in FROM: Contr./ Daisy
> Chain' and 'Data out TO: Term./ Daisy Chain'. Inside is a 2661 with 1488/89
> drivers for each port. Does anybody know if the connection between the host
> and the 'multiplexer' is sort of a processor bus? Any ideas what to use this
> thing for (without the VAX)? Any descriptions of the switches available?
> 

That is part of a family of products. The CS11 controller card (I am assuming
that is a Unibus one) normally could be had with DH11, DMF32, DV11, or maybe
even other emulations. DEpending on various things it supported 32, 48, or 64
ports on those remote panels that could be ~50' away on the 34 wire ribbon
cable. There was a terminator needed at the last panel.

If those panels of 2 x 8 ports are BLACK, they are CP11, if chromate
they are CP12, or if you have any of the CP34 panels they are 16 ports each
and on a per port basis can be optioned for 422 rather than 232 for the 
data leads. These also supported 38.4kb when DEC didn't.

The CP34 panels can run on the same controllers that take the CP11/12
panels (assuming new enough microcode), but NOT intermixed.

Note all the 2901s on the card you have - makes it heavy.

There was another card that ONLY did DMF32 emulation (serial port only,
not printer or SYNC port), but let you have 8 of the CP34 panels for
128 ports.

OTOH, we have the CS04 Q bus card in a MicroVax (only talks to CP34 
panels I suspect) doing DHV emulation that we have run 64 ports on, 
and they are 15 feet away tied only by that one 34 wire ribbon cable.

Way back when in the before terminal server era, this was all 'good-stuff'.

I assume you have the DMF32 emulation, and so I think are limited to 
48 ports with that card. It might also be DH11 emulation with a custom
VMS driver that supported DH11s, and be good for 64 ports. The limit 
was memory, NOT controller CPU power.

If you have the DH11 eproms in it, works VERY WELL on a PDP11 running
things like RSX11M+.

They are not worth much these days, but are VERY SOLID products.


