In article <ROCKWELL.94Mar4103735@nova.umd.edu>, rockwell@nova.umd.edu (Raul Deluth Miller) writes:
> Carl J Lydick:
> .  As best I can tell, the only way for the O/S to "address the social
> .  issues" would be for it to lack the ability to, e.g., restrict disk
> .  usage and process limits.  E.g., I suppose DEC could eliminate disk
> .  quota support from the ACP, and remove the code that supports
> .  enforcement of the UAF parameters MAXJOBS, MAXACCTJOBS, MAXDETACH,
> .  and PRCLM.  That way system managers would have to, as they do with
> .  Unix, hack the system if they wanted these features.  I don't think
> .  that would be a good idea, though.
> 
> Better to set the defaults to unlimited and let the system
> administrators set them when and where unlimited usage is abused.
> Better to include information presented to all users with
> recommendations before enforcing such limits.  Better to treat users
> as competent individuals capable of making rational decisions than as
> utter fools.

	I disagree.  Remember that VMS is targetted for PRODUCTION systems,
not a playground.  One abusive user can disrupt too much for it to be worth
setting everything to unlimited quotas.

	Also, there are some limits that are meant to protect the system
from accidental "run-away" processes.  Some limits are set to best allocate
resources where they can be best used (Providing smaller memory regions
allows more processes, etc).  Setting everything for unlimited could result
too easily hanging the system but over-consumption of resources.  Unix is
not different - you are limited in the number of processes, open files, etc...

> If there's an issue of novice users, they can be given configuration
> information which they can set themselves, once they figure out how.
> This business of straight-jacketing the users is fine if the system
> administrator is the only one doing real work, but fails where the
> system administrator is primarily a manager.

	Unix has some similar contraints, but you don't sqawk about them.
How would you like to be unable to perform your classwork for 3 hours because
of something another student did to eat all the disk, crash the system, etc
whether on purpose or not?  I've been there, and I'd prefer the system to
provide some protection one person keeping many or all users from doing
anything with the system.

> My complaint was where subcommands under "help" did not paginate.

	That means to me that VMS did not know how big your screen was or
even if you were on a CRT.  On Unix you have to set your terminal type
to clue the system about your viewing environment.  VMS is no different.

> .  Try the command:
> 	   $ HELP DIRECTORY EXAMPLES
> .  To list the contents of the current directory and all subdirectories:
> 	   $ DIRECTORY [...]
> .  To list the contents of all directories on the disk other than the main file
> .  directory:
> 	   $ DIRECTORY [*...]
> .  To list the contents of all directories on the disk:
> 	   $ DIRECTORY [000000...]
> 
> I presume the three dots are to be taken literally?  And it's six
> zeros for that last command?  Thank you.

	You're welcome.  The "..." syntax in the directory specification is
a wildcard for directory recursion- i.e. search all directories from that
level on down.  Something that works with VMS commands that take files
for arguments.

> One more question?  How do I get a list of all disks?  For that
> matter, how do I get a list of all configured devices?  [What do I
> need that information for?  Mostly, curiosity.  I can't very well talk
> about a subject intelligently when I have no experience with that
> subject.]

	"$ SHOW DEVICE/MOUNT" typically will provide this for you.  It is
much like a "df", but there are more qualifiers available to get addition
device information as needed.

	"$ SHOW DEVICE" will show all devices on the system (kindof a "ls /dev"
except that everything really exists).

> .  =One thing you can count on:  somebody is going to use the defaults.
> 
> .  Yup.  The defaults are set up to give a reasonable level of
> .  security.  If you use the defaults, it's unlikely that you're going
> .  to have your data corrupted by someone else accidentally.  The
> .  defaults are NOT set at a "tighten down the hatches and lock the
> .  doors" level.  Security on VMS can be made a LOT tighter than the
> .  default configuration.  It can also be made a lot looser.
> 
> I'm talking about read access, not write access.  [Though, the unix
> convention of having a publically writable /tmp is very, very
> convenient.  Ok, so maybe I only have a few meg free on my account --
> I can still bring a large bit of source code into a subdirectory under
> /tmp and compile some major program if I need to.  Maybe I have to
> schedule my time a bit, but it's better than being completely
> stopped.] 

	Sorry, but security means validating read access as well.  Consider
what your reaction would be if full read-access was the default and you
found out that everyone was reading your mail before you learned how to
lock it down yourself.

	As for "extra meg", VMS disk quotas provide for "overdraft" protection
that grants you some disk to allow your to finish your current operation, 
such as finishing saving a file or a compile.  It lets you go over your
limit to some higher value.  Also, I've seen many systems with a "public"
directory much like a /tmp for users to play or used as some other type of
common area.

