In article <CM04y4.HCx@da_vinci.it.uswc.uswest.com>,
Larry Mulcahy <lmulcah@da_vinci.ecte.uswc.uswest.com> wrote:
>
>:   3. Does VMS provide enough bang for the buck?  How does it compare to
>: the competition in this area?
>
>VMS tries to do too much with its layer after layer of needless
>complexity.  The designers of VMS just didn't have the gift for spotting
>the key abstraction to make their design powerful yet simple.
>

Somehow it seems to be lost on you that the layers are a "good" thing.
There's actually some real thought behind them.  This is one of the
few things in our industry where the term "architecture" has real meaning.

The layers allow for a fairly straight-forward, even documented way to
develop your own device drivers and incorporate them into the system -
transparent to the applications!  Take, for example, disk drivers.
You have the straight DEC-supplied device drivers, also add-on drivers
that allow you to implement new features without modifying
applications (e.g., striping/raid & shadowing), RAMdisk drivers,
virtual disk (partitioning) drivers, etc.

*Because* of the layered approach, I can typically use more than one
of these together to access the same device (e.g., DUDRIVER, Striping,
& Shadowing, as well as virutal disks).  There are *documented* methods
for implementing these so that they all can work together (excluding
certain cases where someone didn't program something correctly).

>
>:   9. Does VMS have features that are not found or are not nearly as well
