=>
=>Carl, can you post an example of this?  Let's assume that the record
=>separator is ETX and that you're trying to fill a C structure
=>from each record.  Please post examples in both UNIX and VMS of how
=>to do this.

One way (not necessarily very elegant) to do that sort of thing is:

#include <stdio>
main()
{	char c[8][65];
	int lengths[8], nitems, i, j, x;
	FILE *fp;

	if ((fp = fopen("TEST.DAT", "w", "rfm=stmlf")) == NULL)
	{		perror("Open failed");
			exit(0);
	}
	for(i = 0; i < 8; ++i)
		for(j = 0; j < 64; ++j)
			putc(j, fp);
	fclose(fp);
	if ((fp = fopen("TEST.DAT", "r")) == NULL)
	{	perror("Open failed");
		exit(0);
	}
	for(i = 0; i < 8; ++i)
		for(j = 0; j < 64; ++j)
			if((x = getc(fp)) != EOF)
			{	c[i][j] = x;
				lengths[i] = j + 1;
				if(x == '\003')
					j = 63;
					nitems = i + 1;
			}
			else
			{	if (j != 0)
					nitems = i + 1;
				i = 8;
				j = 63;
			}
	fclose(fp);
	printf("%d\n", nitems);
	for(i = 0; i < nitems; ++i)
		printf("%d\n", lengths[i]);
}

The above is the VMS version.  For the unix version, just remove the string
	, "rfm=stmlf"
from the first fopen() call.
