In article <1994Mar3.152134.1@levy.fnal.gov>,
Mark E. Levy, ext. 8056 <levy@levy.fnal.gov> wrote:

	...

>If you're going to state an OPINION, say so.  As stated in a previous post,
>UNIX is a system written by programmers for programmers.  If you're a
>programmer who wishes to spend your time hacking the O/S, instead of
>programming usefull things, UNIX is great.  For real productivity, use VMS.

Considering the amount of time that I have spent recently fixing DEC VMS
environment problems compared to the time I have spent fixing UNIX environment
problems and that my primary assignment is UNIX environment support, I find
the statement `for real productivity, use VMS' very hard to believe.  The
logical complexity and lack of user level facilities and documentation are
DEC VMS' worse faults, in my eyes.  I find the DEC HELP system awful.

Argument of when should file wildcarding be done is similar to when should
linking of program functions be done -- when the program is written,
preprocessing time, compile time, linkage time, program load time,
during program execution, or at envocation time.  There are good reasons
for each of these times and historically there has been a tendency to move
later and and later.  UNIX normally does its wildcarding at command line
intperpreter time; and, DEC VMS, at envocation time.  Both operating systems
have facilities to move the time.

My version of his statement ("If you're a programmer who wishes to spend your
time hacking the O/S, instead of programming usefull things, UNIX is great.
For real productivity, use VMS.") is: If you are person who constructs
programs from facilities provided by programmers for a small range of
purposes and machine hardware efficiency is a major concern, then use DEC
VMS or other main frame class operating system.  If a very flexible operating
system is needed, UNIX is a better choice.  Most of the physicists at Fermilab
appear to me to be in the first classification.  And, in my experience, this
is true of the vast majority of all computer users.

I find Mark Levy's statement, "instead of programming usefull [sic] things"
very perjorative.

>Here at Fermilab, a great deal of the code that ran on our expired Amdahl, and
>the code that runs on our UNIX systems, is written on a VAX and ported over.

This statement is both true and somewhat misleading.  Much of the development
work for these programs occured much earlier on a variety of machines, e.g.
CDC computers under the Network Operating system (NOS) or even on IBM 709s
under IBSYS (where the first FORTRAN was developed).  They were more recently
ported to DEC VAX under DEC VMS.  Furthermore, for an extended period of time,
the programs were ported to the DEC environment in the absence of either the
Amdahl (IBM VM) or the UNIX(reg.tm) environment.  Causality alone is all that
is needed to explain why the porting of these programs was from the DEC to
either the IBM VM or the UNIX environments instead of some other direction.

I did the Amdahl benchmarks twice.  The first time I was given CDC NOS source
to be converted.  The second time I was given DEC VMS sources.  Each time I
was told that the sources where representative of the programs and the systems
used at Fermilab.  The first benchmark qualified but was not successful. A
CDC machine was bought.  The second benchmark was successful.  An Amdahl was
bought (through Systems House).

The largest problem in the second conversion was the removal of the VAXisms
from the benchmark code.  It appears to me that the Fermilab programmers
(usually the physicists themselves) must either be color-blind, ignorant of
the significance of the black (standard) and blue (DEC extension) or did not
understand the high maintenance cost of using extensions specific to a single
vendor.  And, historically, high energy physics has been multiple vendor.

>And yes, this is MY opinion.

From previous conversations with Mark Levy, I can and do believe that this
is a correct statement of his opinion.

What Mark Levy did not tell you is that his background is mostly DEC VMS with
little UNIX experience.  My experience is the opposite.

