And I thought I would avoid this religious war...  For the record, I
make my living programming predominantly in VMS, but I alse use Unix
and believe that each environment has strengths and weaknesses.

In article 8sM@da_vinci.it.uswc.uswest.com, lmulcah@da_vinci.ecte.uswc.uswest.com (Larry Mulcahy) writes:

>Those of you arguing in favor of VMS wildcards are desperately wrong.
>There are a couple of issues here so let me take them one at a time.
>
>(1) The shell.  In VMS you get DCL.  With Unix your vendor probably
>gives you sh, csh and ksh, and if you aren't satisfied there's bash,
>tcsh and others.  So we have the VMS way (bondage) vs. the Unix way
>(freedom).  

You're fudging history here to make a point.  Most of those shells were
written by "users" who didn't like came with the operating system and
thought they could "program" something better.  The fact that you can
get all these neat new shells are not because of AT+T's emphasis, or
a direction that Sun was pushing.  Rather, that most academians tend to
share their new programs with others on the network.  Sure commercial
sellers now include some of these other shells.  They're free, and the
company has included it with their Unix as a value-added aspect.

There's nothing stopping "users" from writing their own shell under VMS.
Just because there isn't a plethora of shells for VMS mean that the OS
is deficeint.  On the contrary, I'd say that indicates that DCL is pretty
robust, and that "users" tend to like it.  (At least enough not to write
their own shell language.)
>
>(2) Wildcards.  It's true that you could get a Unix shell to do it the
>way DCL does, but this would be a mistake.  The VMS solution is for any
>program that needs to be able to handle wildcards on the command line to
>be written that way.  Some programs are written that way, other's
>aren't.  So generally, the effects of
>
>    $ program *.*
>
>(once you've gone through that burdensome business of defining a foreign
>command) are unpredictable.  One program will read every file in the
>current directory, another will just think you're trying to do something
>to a file named `*.*'.

What happens in Unix if I don't want *.* to represent all files?  It strikes
me that your shell will expand it to something that means nothing to my
program.  VMS lets my program determine what is the appropriate interpretation
of that parameter.

Hmm, I think I can feel the blast of a rebuttal coming my way.  You know,
OS wars (like language wars) can never be resolved unless there is a clearly
superior side.  I don't see this in the VMS/UNIX debate, and I don't think
I ever will.

