>Yes.  The writing is on the wall for proprietary operating systems like
>VMS.  One after another the companies who used this strategy have been
>forced to let go of their proprietary OSes or go out of business.  Only
>the large installed base of VMS has allowed it to cling to life until
>now.

I can't believe we are still arguing about this.

VMS isn't dying because UNIX is better (it isn't). Operating systems can
only be compared in relation to the purpose you want to use them for, and
it's clear that for many purposes, VMS performs brilliantly. No, VMS is
dying because DEC's apalling marketing didn't give it enough chance to
succeed.

Operating Systems, like everything else, get one chance to make it. If they
don't succeed within a year or two, they die. Sometimes they have limited
success, and keep going for a while, and VMS has done pretty well, but it
can't compete with UNIX because UNIX was marketed in a better way (yes, it
was by accident rather than design, but the results are the same).

Look at VHS. Look at the 6502. Look at the 8086. Look at DOS/Windows. None
of these are technically the best system, but they were marketed better, and
they took over the market because of that.

DEC have had the fastest processor in the world for about two years now, and
a modern, well-designed implementation of UNIX, all at reasonable prices.
They still can't sell it. Why? Because their marketing has *always* been a
piece of sh*t, whereas their technical skills are some of the best in the
market. In contrast, MS sells millions of copies of a piece of sh*t.

Systems don't succeed because they're technically better. They succeed for
other reasons. Talk to Steve Jobs if you don't believe me.

Just my opinion. Please don't flame me, I don't like it.

	Chris Downey


