Its been my experience that when you give a VMS command using wildcards,
e.g., DIR *.COM, it works in a consistent fashion for a given command. I
cannot count on 'chmod 755 *' working under any Unix shell I've seen.  How
does "ksh: /bin/chmod: arg list too long" sound to you?  If the shell is
going to expand wildcards it should do so without error.

