As best I can tell, the only way for the O/S to "address the social issues"
would be for it to lack the ability to, e.g., restrict disk usage and process
limits.  E.g., I suppose DEC could eliminate disk quota support from the ACP,
and remove the code that supports enforcement of the UAF parameters MAXJOBS,
MAXACCTJOBS, MAXDETACH, and PRCLM.  That way system managers would have to, as
they do with Unix, hack the system if they wanted these features.  I don't
think that would be a good idea, though.

=.  > [That, and all to often I run into parts of the help system that are
=.  > unable to send information a screen at a time.  Which can be
=.  > inconvenient, or crippling, depending on my situation at the time.]
=
=	   I'm not sure this is a typo or not, but it is easily possible to
=.  extract the text of a help library and read/edit it as you wish.  Much like
=.  making your own copy of a "man" page...  Again, this is probably from
=.  lack of familiarity, but that does not make it a fault.  If it is, then
=.  UNIX is also full of faults, since your average DOS user is completely
=.  lost, much like you may feel on VMS.

I think the complaint was about programs that use LBR$OUTPUT_HELP and don't
supply their own output routine.  By default, if you use LBR$OUTPUT_HELP, you
don't get pagination.  Of course, for most unix commands, you don't get ANY
help from within the program, other than a "Usage..." line if you get the
syntax wrong.

=	   It sounds like you might have been suffering from the same system
=.  admins that give you small quotas.  DEC has lots of documentation available,
=.  but your admins may not have it or allow you to have it.  In the directory
=.  tree SYS$EXAMPLES (DECW$EXAMPLES for X/Motif stuff) are a wide variety of
=.  examples, as well as example program listings in much of the documentation
=.  (again, something which you may never have been given access to).
=
=Then again, I never encountered a piece of documentation in the
=on-line help [all that I could afford to access] which described the
=directory tree.

Try the command:
	$ HELP DIRECTORY EXAMPLES

=However, if someone gives me an idea of what sort of command I could
=type in on a VMS system to get this piece of information, I'll write
=it down and save it -- in case I ever get back on a VMS system that
=happens to allow such an operation.

To list the contents of the current directory and all subdirectories:
	$ DIRECTORY [...]
To list the contents of all directories on the disk other than the main file
directory:
	$ DIRECTORY [*...]
To list the contents of all directories on the disk:
	$ DIRECTORY [000000...]

=True.  The configuration isn't set in the assembly language that makes
=up the O/S.
=
=However, a system administrator is generally not going to know that a
=problem needs to be dealt with until the problem is made apparent.  A
=random student is going to learn a lot more about an operating system
=that was built with a "wide-open-share-everything" philosophy than
=about an operating system that was built with a "tighten down the
=hatches and lock the doors" philosophy.

One thing the random student isn't going to learn about on a system that has
virtually no security is security.

=One thing you can count on:  somebody is going to use the defaults.

Yup.  The defaults are set up to give a reasonable level of security.  If you
use the defaults, it's unlikely that you're going to have your data corrupted
by someone else accidentally.  The defaults are NOT set at a "tighten down the
hatches and lock the doors" level.  Security on VMS can be made a LOT tighter
than the default configuration.  It can also be made a lot looser.
