Carl J Lydick:
.  As best I can tell, the only way for the O/S to "address the social
.  issues" would be for it to lack the ability to, e.g., restrict disk
.  usage and process limits.  E.g., I suppose DEC could eliminate disk
.  quota support from the ACP, and remove the code that supports
.  enforcement of the UAF parameters MAXJOBS, MAXACCTJOBS, MAXDETACH,
.  and PRCLM.  That way system managers would have to, as they do with
.  Unix, hack the system if they wanted these features.  I don't think
.  that would be a good idea, though.

Better to set the defaults to unlimited and let the system
administrators set them when and where unlimited usage is abused.
Better to include information presented to all users with
recommendations before enforcing such limits.  Better to treat users
as competent individuals capable of making rational decisions than as
utter fools.

If there's an issue of novice users, they can be given configuration
information which they can set themselves, once they figure out how.
This business of straight-jacketing the users is fine if the system
administrator is the only one doing real work, but fails where the
system administrator is primarily a manager.

.  I think the complaint was about programs that use LBR$OUTPUT_HELP
.  and don't supply their own output routine.  By default, if you use
.  LBR$OUTPUT_HELP, you don't get pagination.  Of course, for most
.  unix commands, you don't get ANY help from within the program,
.  other than a "Usage..." line if you get the syntax wrong.

My complaint was where subcommands under "help" did not paginate.  I
was drawing an analogy between vms "help" and unix "man".  [On the one
hand, neither provide complete information.  On the other hand, both
expect you to have some understanding of the system in formulating
queries.  On the third hand, it seems harder to deduce how to
formulate such queries by examining other parts of the vms system.]   
.  
.  Try the command:
	   $ HELP DIRECTORY EXAMPLES
.  To list the contents of the current directory and all subdirectories:
	   $ DIRECTORY [...]
.  To list the contents of all directories on the disk other than the main file
.  directory:
	   $ DIRECTORY [*...]
.  To list the contents of all directories on the disk:
	   $ DIRECTORY [000000...]

I presume the three dots are to be taken literally?  And it's six
zeros for that last command?  Thank you.

One more question?  How do I get a list of all disks?  For that
matter, how do I get a list of all configured devices?  [What do I
need that information for?  Mostly, curiosity.  I can't very well talk
about a subject intelligently when I have no experience with that
subject.]

.  One thing the random student isn't going to learn about on a system
.  that has virtually no security is security.

Maybe.  Then again "virtually no security" is rather ill-defined.

.  =One thing you can count on:  somebody is going to use the defaults.

.  Yup.  The defaults are set up to give a reasonable level of
.  security.  If you use the defaults, it's unlikely that you're going
.  to have your data corrupted by someone else accidentally.  The
.  defaults are NOT set at a "tighten down the hatches and lock the
.  doors" level.  Security on VMS can be made a LOT tighter than the
.  default configuration.  It can also be made a lot looser.

I'm talking about read access, not write access.  [Though, the unix
convention of having a publically writable /tmp is very, very
convenient.  Ok, so maybe I only have a few meg free on my account --
I can still bring a large bit of source code into a subdirectory under
/tmp and compile some major program if I need to.  Maybe I have to
schedule my time a bit, but it's better than being completely
stopped.] 

