> machine.  "Better kept secrets" aren't going to make things easier for
> the people who have to use the machine.  

	It sounds like you might have been suffering from the same system
admins that give you small quotas.  DEC has lots of documentation available,
but your admins may not have it or allow you to have it.  In the directory
tree SYS$EXAMPLES (DECW$EXAMPLES for X/Motif stuff) are a wide variety of
examples, as well as example program listings in much of the documentation
(again, something which you may never have been given access to).

	But you do have a valid point:  DEC's marketting has done a lousy
job for many years.

> [That, and all to often I run into parts of the help system that are
> unable to send information a screen at a time.  Which can be
> inconvenient, or crippling, depending on my situation at the time.]

	I'm not sure this is a typo or not, but it is easily possible to
extract the text of a help library and read/edit it as you wish.  Much like
making your own copy of a "man" page...  Again, this is probably from
lack of familiarity, but that does not make it a fault.  If it is, then
UNIX is also full of faults, since your average DOS user is completely
lost, much like you may feel on VMS.

>    >>  9. Does VMS have features that are not found or are not nearly
>    >>as well developed in competing operating systems?  If so, what
>    >>are they?
>    > File ACLs, Accounting, Resource control......
>      ACL's on everything, Process Class scheduling (V6.0), file
>      structures, I/O through the command language, VMSclusters,
>      locking ($ENQ), logicals names, ...
> 
> Which might be why VMS systems are so hard for a person to use --
> there's all this administrative bullshit you have to go through to get
> permission to use the system.  Better to use a system that relies on
> social mechanisms than one which relies on red tape.

	Again you type this almost exact same text.  Since when does the
existence of a facility mean that it is "red tape" and "administrative
bullshit", unless you are talking about the actual PEOPLE that are
running the system - something that is independent of the operating system.

	I/O via DCL is not red tape.  ACL's are not red tape, since users
are the ones to control ACL's on their files to control who has access to
their data (as it should be).  Class Scheduling allows management (yes, the
people who actually own the machine) to divide the resources in order to meet
business needs.  Locking is not "administrative bullshit" but a mechanism
built-in to assist in providing interlocks mechanisms to prevent corruption
of data.

> Micro-management is not necessarily better management.  And armor
> plating is not always the best approach to security.

	This is true, but VMS does not manage the users, but provides
facilities for the users to manage their resources/files/etc...  The
"armor plating" is available as needed for those sites that require it,
but I suspect that the armor plating you've been running into is people-
created, not O/S (UNIX or otherwise) created.

