In article <2l3q0d$n75@morrow.stanford.edu>, salem@pangea.Stanford.EDU (Bruce Salem) writes:
> In article <2MAR199410443262@jhuvms.hcf.jhu.edu> ecf_stbo@jhuvms.hcf.jhu.edu (look out, here he comes again) writes:
> >In article <2l0k63$5a6@pyrtech.mis.pyramid.com>, bsalem@pyrtech.mis.pyramid.com (Bruce Salem) writes...
> >>	My experience was in writing a F77 (DEC-FORTRAN) application
> >>in 1987 that I had to know the modes of the file to get it to open,
> >
> >Sorry, but what are you talking about? What are the modes of a file?
> 
> 	I had to know more than the data file was an ascii file, I had
> to know if it was sequential or random access, and I had to know its
> logical record length. I didn't always know this information in advance
> and I didn't have a good way to find it out.

But surely that is an application issue?  You don't need to know that
simply to get the data out of the file. RMS will determine the file
structure and extract the data appropriately when you issue a read.

If tbings like record length are important to your application that is
of course perfectly normal, but is equally true on any OS. I think
the point being made originally was that with RMS you simply say
'get me a record', and it will. It reads the file header to determine
what a record is for that file structure, and returns the appropriate
data to your read request.  Things like sequential/random access refer to
the way your application pulls out records, not to the underlying
filesystem.

Steve


