wws@sorcerer.isp.bellcore.com (Wayne Scott) writes:


>	I'm about to transition back to the VMS environment after 6 years of
>X/Motif on AIX and Alpha OSF/1.  I'm trying to get a feel for how
different
>DECwindows might be from what I've been used to.  Several recent
articles
>in this group suggested that DECwindows is actually Motif.  But, when
I
>scanned a DECwindows manual several months ago, it looked like there
were
>a bunch of VMS-type calls (thought I can't remember now what I saw).
>Can anyone give me some pointers about what to expect?
>
  "DECwindows" was originally coined as the name of the project that
  eventually delivered the initial version of X11/XUI on VMS and
  Ultrix.  The term today is equally vaugue in terms of exactly what
  it applies to.  XUI was a API that was developed before there was
  a standard toolkit.  It was replaced by Motif (although there is
  backward support for XUI and it's window manager).

  VMS systems today come with the X11 server, fonts, drivers, and
  other server-side bits on the base system.  They are installed as
  the "DECwindows" savesets.  The client-side bits, including the
  libraries, include files, window manager, OOTB applications are
  part of a seperate "Motif" layered product.

  The normal C binding for Xlib/Motif is provided, as well as a
  VMS standard binding to allow other VMS languages to call X11
  routines without jumping through C binding hoops.

  In short, the X11 part of your application code will run on Unix
  and VMS - you just need to watch out for Unix-isms like forking,
  unless you use the POSIX environment.

